BBC News,Western Europe,en,Russia says seven killed and 40 injured by Ukrainian drone hitting busy beach,Russia says seven killed and 40 injured by Ukrainian drone hitting busy beach,"Russia says seven killed and 40 injured by Ukrainian drone hitting busy beach Three children were killed when the drone crashed at a popular resort on the Black Sea. A drone hit a busy beach in southern Russia, killing seven people including three children, local authorities have said. In social media footage verified by the BBC, a drone can be seen hurtling towards a cliffside before crashing into a fireball on the beach below as tourists look on from the shore and in the water. The total number of injured reached 40, with 17 people hospitalised, said the governor of the Krasnodar region Veniamin Kondratyev. He accused Ukraine of ""a deliberate strike on civilians"". Ukraine has not commented. Both sides deny targeting civilians deliberately. It is unclear what the drone was targeting. Some Ukrainian Telegram channels have claimed it crashed onto the beach as a result of electronic warfare which jammed the drone's signal. Several eyewitnesses were quoted by Russian media as saying gunfire preceded the explosion, indicating that air defences may have tried to shoot the drone down. The crash occurred on a beach near the village of Arkhipo-Osipovka on the Black Sea. With its warm climate, long beaches and developed tourist infrastructure it is a popular holiday destination for Russians, in particular since sanctions have curtailed international travel for many. Tourists and residents of Arkhipo-Osipovka told local media outlets that no sirens sounded ahead of the drone attack. ""We covered ourselves with sun loungers, and didn't see where it exploded,"" said one holidaymaker. Another person said that while they did not see the drone hit they co","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 18:04:30 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cr7kmnyrdn7o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:12
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Iran says it is in talks with Oman but not the US after Trump says negotiations to resume,Iran says it is in talks with Oman but not the US after Trump says negotiations to resume,"Iran says it is in talks with Oman but not the US after Trump says negotiations to resume Iran's remarks that there were no talks with the US prompts Donald Trump to issue a strong condemnation of its leadership. Iran says it is negotiating with Oman to reach ""an understanding"" over a temporary route through the Strait of Hormuz to allow commercial shipping to resume. The statement came after US President Donald Trump said new talks on ending the war in Iran would begin on Monday - and that he had called off ""massive"" strikes on the country for this purpose. Trump did not specify who would take part in the talks. Iran, however, said it was not negotiating with the US - and had no plans to do so. Tehran's denial appears to have irked the US president, who later wrote on his Truth Social media platform that Iran's leadership was ""unbelievably duplicitous"". Speaking to reporters at the White House later, he insisted talks were in fact taking place. ""They're going on right now... this is the last chance for them to sign a good document,"" he said. At the weekend, Trump had said the ""perimeters"" of a deal had been agreed - and would include reopening Hormuz, the vital waterway whose closure by Iran has sent oil prices soaring. Tehran has repeatedly closed off the strait since the US and Israel started attacks on the country in February. Speaking on board Air Force One on Sunday, he said Iran ""knew the extent"" of the planned US strikes ""because they saw it forming"". Trump declined to outline a deadline for a deal to be struck with Tehran, adding: ""We'll just see how it is. We're ready to go anytime we want... I'm not looking to kill people."" Trump added that Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince Moham","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 19:42:55 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c23579jzv08o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:12
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Threat to oil tankers in Middle East  worst since start of Iran war, analysts say","Threat to oil tankers in Middle East  worst since start of Iran war, analysts say","Threat to oil tankers in Middle East  worst since start of Iran war, analysts say Fresh attacks on alternative shipping routes have left tankers facing an increasingly complex situation, analysts say. The threat to ships carrying oil in the Middle East is at its worst since the Iran war started, experts have said, after a spate of attacks on another key route in the region. The warning comes after a number of attacks on vessels in the Red Sea, an alternative waterway that some tankers had been using since Iran blocked the Strait of Hormuz. Iran has denied Donald Trump's claimthat it was in talks with the US over reopening the strait, but said it was speaking to Oman about securing the shipping lane. ""In terms of threat to the trade of crude, we're at the worst period that we've been in since this since this crisis began,"" said Matthew Wright, an analyst at ship-tracking firm Kpler. The number of ships passing throughthe Strait of Hormuzwas just eight on Sunday and 11 on Saturday, compared with more than 100 per day before the war began, according to Kpler. Before the conflict, about 20% of the world's oil and gas passed through the strait. The temporary peace deal with the US struck in early June led to numbers rising again, but the resumption of strikes between the two countries roughly a month later have significantly reduced traffic. Many ships have been ""going dark"" when crossing the strait, meaning they turn off their transponder to avoid detection. For much of the war, some ships carrying oil from Saudi Arabia had instead passed through an alternative shipping lane in the Red Sea, between the kingdom and northeast Africa. But a recentspate of attacks by Yemen's Houth","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 15:57:27 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cjrv0dy2e90o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:12
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Rhine falls to record low levels as drought strains Europe's rivers,Rhine falls to record low levels as drought strains Europe's rivers,"Rhine falls to record low levels as drought strains Europe's rivers Low water levels in the Rhine, Danube and Po have curtailed the transport of goods and reduced electricity output. The River Rhine has fallen to record low levels in several locations in Germany and the Netherlands as prolonged heatwaves continue across Europe. Last week, levels were measured in Cologne in western Germany and in Lobith, where the Rhine crosses from Germany into the Netherlands, that were lower than at any point since records began. Other major European rivers with important transport, industrial and agricultural functions have also seen record low levels, including the Danube, which flows through 10 countries, and Italy's Po river. Drought conditions remained stable and critical in most of Europe and worsened in central-western Europe in mid-July, according to the EU's Drought Observatories. Renewed heatwave conditions are due to continue for at least another week, pushing temperatures above 40C in many central and southern European countries. Germany's Low Water Information System (NIWIS) said that 44% of the Rhine measuring stations and 78% of those on the Danube had recorded extremely low water levels at the end of July. The Rhine - which starts in the Swiss Alps and empties in the North Sea - is one of Europe's busiest waterways. Decreasing water levels have sparked concerns about the economic fallout of having to limit navigation and shipping. On Friday, the navigable water depth at the chokepoint of Kaub near Koblenz hit the 2018 record low of 25cm, before recovering slightly on Monday. The Kaub gauging station in western Germany is a key indicator of navigability o","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 16:27:43 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c78gn8zvrx4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:12
BBC News,Western Europe,en,US reports first two deaths linked to  'explosive diarrhoea' outbreak,US reports first two deaths linked to  'explosive diarrhoea' outbreak,"US reports first two deaths linked to  'explosive diarrhoea' outbreak The illness caused by parasitic infection that spreads through contaminated food or water is not generally life-threatening, health officials say. Michigan health officials have confirmed the first two deaths linked to the ongoing cyclospora outbreak, a parasitic infection spread through contaminated food or water that can cause severe diarrhoea. The two individuals had ""significant underlying health conditions that may have been impacted by cyclosporiasis and dehydration"", state health authorities said. The foodborne illness has spread to 45 states, with nearly 7,000 laboratory-confirmed cases reported nationwide, according to the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC). While the outbreak has infected thousands of people, the CDC says deaths from cyclosporiasis are far from common, as it is ""not usually life-threatening"". Michigan's health department did not provide further details about those who have died. The state is at the epicentre of the current outbreak and its records show more than 11,000 cases and 193 hospitalisations, although many have not been laboratory-confirmed. The heat-loving parasite infects the intestines and is transmitted through faecal contamination. Past outbreaks have occurred when people consumed fruits or vegetables irrigated with water contaminated by faeces. The CDC recommends washing all produce under running water, including those that are labelled as pre-washed. But washing alone might not be enough to rid produce of the parasite. Cooking produce to a temperature of at least 70°C (158 °F) can kill the parasite, the agency recommends. Officials have linked this year's widesp","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 20:05:46 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c2k7px317eeo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:12
BBC News,Western Europe,en,EU calls for stronger borders after Ceuta migrant crossings,EU calls for stronger borders after Ceuta migrant crossings,"EU calls for stronger borders after Ceuta migrant crossings Ursula von der Leyen has called for action ahead of an urgent EU interior ministers meeting to discuss the issue. European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen has called for ""united action"" on border security after the chaotic influx of tens of thousands of migrants from Morocco into the Spanish exclave of Ceuta last week. Spain ⁠estimates that about 69,500 migrants ​have now returned to Morocco, exceeding initial estimates of 50,000. The official death toll on the Spanish side of the border stands at 72, with 11 deaths on the Moroccan side. On Tuesday, EU interior ministers will have emergency talks by video conference to discuss the issue. The scenes in Ceuta have strained relations among some of the EU's 27 member states. In a letter to Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez, von der Leyen praised his ""swift handling"" of the crisis. But she added: ""From this incident, it is clear that we must do more to further strengthen our borders at critical points."" In response to the Ceuta crossings, Italy temporarily suspended the Schengen arrangement with Spain. The Schengen agreement eliminates border checks and now encompasses more than 450 million people and 29 countries in Europe. Finland and Denmark backed Italy's move, while Czech Prime Minister Andrej Babiš urged a temporary suspension of Spain's Schengen membership. In response, Sánchez said he had ""serious concerns"" about some European governments and pointed out that Ceuta was not part of the Schengen area. While most had shown ""support and solidarity"", he said other European governments had chosen to attack Spain, ""driven by pre","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 21:43:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cyvl84zmgyro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Infantino opponents threaten 'non co-operation' with Fifa,Infantino opponents threaten 'non co-operation' with Fifa,"Infantino opponents threaten 'non co-operation' with Fifa Opponents of Fifa president Gianni Infantino are threatening ""non co-operation"" with the governing body unless he steps down, according to senior Uefa sources. Opponents of Fifa president Gianni Infantino are threatening ""non co-operation"" with the governing body unless he steps down, according to senior Uefa sources. Some of those involved in the rebellion vow ""there is no going back"" for as long as Infantino remains in position, as the fallout over hisscrapped plans to sell off stakes in Fifa competitionsto private investors intensifies. While the details of the proposed disruption remain unclear, one senior figure said they would ""certainly expect"" a refusal to participate in Fifa meetings. It comes after European football's governing body Uefa threatened legal action over the proposals, andWales publicly withdrew backingfor Infantino's bid for a fourth term as Fifa president, with England's Football Association set to follow suit. In March, Infantino is set to stand for re-election for a final term as president and would need 106 votes from Fifa's 211 members to win. Sources have told BBC Sport the Scottish FA's position on the future of Infantino remains aligned with Uefa's lack of confidence in the 56-year-old. Infantino's leadership had already beenpublicly questionedby football's governing bodies in Europe, North and Central America and the Caribbean, with Uefa and Concacaf releasing statements criticising the plans. As the pressure grows on Infantino with Serbia, Sweden and Finland also withdrawing their support, Morocco, Qatar, Lebanon, Kuwait and Sri Lanka among the countries to have pledged their support for his Fifa lea","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 07:49:33 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cp30vg829nxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Aung San Suu Kyi appears healthy in first confirmed outside contact for 2.5 years,Aung San Suu Kyi appears healthy in first confirmed outside contact for 2.5 years,"Aung San Suu Kyi appears healthy in first confirmed outside contact for 2.5 years Photos newly released by Myanmar's military government show Suu Kyi meeting with an ICRC official. Detained Burmese leader Aung San Suu Kyi has been allowed to meet a delegation from the International Committee of the Red Cross, the first confirmed outside contact with her since a letter to her son two and a half years ago. In photographs of the meeting released by the military government Suu Kyi appeared to be healthy. She was detained shortly after the armed forces deposed her government in 2021, and sentenced her to 33 years in jail on what are widely viewed as trumped-up charges, thoughher sentence has been reducedandshe has been moved to house arrest. Despite repeated assurances from the military that Suu Kyi is alive and well, they have never provided convincing evidence of this. Her son Kim Aris has in recent months mounted an international campaign demanding proof of life for the 81-year-old democracy icon. Now a delegation from the ICRC has been allowed to meet her – her first confirmed meeting with anyone outside the government for nearly four years. Photographs released of her with the ICRC team led by its representative Arnaud de Baecque show Suu Kyi looking relaxed and well, although no details of her state of health have been given, nor of what they talked about. ""The visit was carried out in accordance with the ICRC's standards and procedures for visiting people deprived of liberty. This included the opportunity to speak with Daw Aung San Suu Kyi in private,"" the ICRC said in a short statement. Kim Aris welcomed the news, saying the pictures appear real and ","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 14:20:56 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c1e1d5j6660o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Two workers killed in blast after re-entering quake-hit Japan mall on boss' order,Two workers killed in blast after re-entering quake-hit Japan mall on boss' order,"Two workers killed in blast after re-entering quake-hit Japan mall on boss' order The two women's employer has apologised for sending them back to Aeon Mall to secure cash. Two workers who died in a shopping mall explosion that followed a powerful earthquake in Japan last week were asked by a boss to re-enter the building to secure cash. Their employer, general goods store Habita, has apologised for the ""loss of two precious lives"". The two were among 38 people who died in the magnitude 6.8 earthquake in Kumamoto prefecture on 28 July. Seven of the fatalities were from Aeon Mall. ""The instructions given at the time were inappropriate, and I truly regret them,"" Habita sales director Goji Yuse said, NHK reported. One of two workers has been named as 22-year-old Kurumi Otake. The identity of the other deceased employee has not been made public. Otake's family told local media that she ran into a relative outside the Aeon Mall after the earthquake. She told the relative that she had to go back in because her company had asked her to move money from the cash register into a safe. This was confirmed by two company representatives, who attended Otake'a wake on Sunday and apologised to her family. He added that the company was considering how to proceed with the matter, NHK reported. ""Looking back now, it was not worth a life,"" Yuse told reporters at the wake, Kyodo reported. Otake's relative described her as someone with a ""strong sense of justice"". She had supported her mother after her father died three years ago, Asahi Shimbun reported. ""If she hadn't gone back inside the store at that time, Kurumi would still be with her family today,"" the relative","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 08:40:22 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c24mr09r99eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Russian anti-war politician says he has fled the country,Russian anti-war politician says he has fled the country,"Russian anti-war politician says he has fled the country Boris Nadezhdin, who was recently designated as a foreign agent, posted a video in front of the Eiffel Tower. An anti-war politician who tried to challenge Vladimir Putin at the last Russian presidential election says he has fled the country after Moscow declared him a ""foreign agent"" last month. Boris Nadezhdin posted a video standing in front of the Eiffel Tower in Paris. He said: ""Hi everyone. I have some good news. I'm alive and free. Unfortunately, I'm not in Russia at the moment."" Nadezhdin, 63, came to prominence two years ago when he tried to stand for the presidency on an anti-war platform, but was barred because electoral authorities ruled signatures he had submitted were flawed. He was designated a ""foreign agent"" by the justice ministry last month after saying he would run as a candidate in September's Duma (parliamentary) elections. Nadezhdin was accused of spreading false information about the Russian government and calling on people to take part in unauthorised rallies. The label would have prevented him from taking part in the elections. He was charged with ""displaying extremist symbols"" – a reference to an image of the late opposition leader Alexei Navalny, which appeared for 10 seconds in a video reposted by Nadezhdin's social media accounts in November 2023. He called the charge ridiculous. Last week, he told BBC Russian from an undisclosed location that his prospects were ""vague"" after he was forced to abandon his campaign to run for parliament. ""If you're a politician, you should participate in politics while you have the opportunity. How do you participate in po","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 15:41:08 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c70gjw60033o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,US and Japan take action to prop up yen in rare joint move,US and Japan take action to prop up yen in rare joint move,"US and Japan take action to prop up yen in rare joint move Both countries have said that they will not hesitate to conduct joint interventions in the future. Japan and the US have confirmed that they jointly intervened last week to halt a slide in the yen after it fell to a fresh 40-year low. The joint intervention is the first since 2011, when both countries took coordinated action to weaken the yen after the devastating earthquake and tsunami that hit eastern Japan. Both Japan's Ministry of Finance and US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent have said that they will not hesitate to conduct more joint interventions in the future. It highlights both countries' efforts to prevent a sell-off in the yen and Japanese government bonds from having an impact on the global economy, including potentially helping to push up borrowing costs for Washington. Bank of Japan data indicated that Tokyo may have sold almost $59bn of US dollars to buy yen when it intervened in New York markets on Thursday, before Friday's confirmed joint intervention with Washington. The US has not confirmed the size of its intervention, but a Reuters photograph of a notepad in front of Bessent during a cabinet meeting on Friday read: ""To Do: Buy Japanese Yen $5-10 bil"". Shigeto Nagai, head of Japan economics at Oxford Economics, told the BBC: ""The United States agreed to participate in the coordinated intervention because it serves its national interests by offering the prospect of significant benefits at a low cost."" The two countries are expected to continue to intervene ""intermittently in a coordinated manner for some time"", he added. ""Even if the actual amount of in","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 07:30:52 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cglj1pr0wjwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,"Sudan army drone strike on court session kills 35, rights group says","Sudan army drone strike on court session kills 35, rights group says","Sudan army drone strike on court session kills 35, rights group says The army has not commented on claims that civilians died in the attack on a paramilitary-held village. Thirty-five people were killed in a drone strike by the Sudanese army on a traditional court session in the north of the Darfur region, a local rights organisation has said. Dozens more were reported injured in Sunday afternoon's attack. The Emergency Lawyers Group, which monitors violence against civilians, said the attack occurred in Ghara al-Zawiya village that is controlled by the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF). The army has not commented. The RSF holds most of Darfur, in the west of the country, as the two sides continue to fight in the three-year-long civil war that has devastated the country, killed tens of thousands of people and forced millions from their homes. Sunday's ""court session was being held under a tree with the participation of local community leaders,"" a witness is quoted by the AFP news agency as saying. Four tribal leaders and two RSF commanders were among those killed in the attack, according to the witness. The privately owned Darfur 24 website quoted a local resident who said that two drones flew over the town ""before launching more than four missiles"". The Emergency Lawyers Group called for an investigation into the drone strike and condemned the targeting of the court saying that attacks on civilians and civilian infrastructure are a ""grave violation of international humanitarian law"". It also noted that drones are being increasingly used ""to target civilian areas and gatherings, which exacerbates the risks faced by the population"". The RS","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 10:29:24 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ce85097leydo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'My only son was shot standing up for justice' - inside city gripped by deadly protests in Pakistan-administered Kashmir,'My only son was shot standing up for justice' - inside city gripped by deadly protests in Pakistan-administered Kashmir,"'My only son was shot standing up for justice' - inside city gripped by deadly protests in Pakistan-administered Kashmir The BBC gains unique access to Rawalakot, scene of violent clashes between security forces and protesters in Pakistan-administered Kashmir. Dr Anwar's makeshift clinic is not far from the main protest site in the city of Rawalakot in Pakistan-administered Kashmir (PAK). A spare bedroom acts as a medical supply cupboard, outside is a bed under a red stretched tarpaulin. Lying across the bed is Shafqat Hussain, shot in the chest. The sheet he lies on is already stained with the blood of previously injured. He is dying. In the last two months, dozens of people have been killed across the region in clashes between security officials and protesters from the group Joint Awami Action Committee (JAAC). This region - home to about 4.5 million people -currently has elections taking place. The BBC is the first media organisation to gain access to Rawalakot and speak to those at the heart of the protests. Dr Anwar – whose name, like most of those in this report, has been changed to protect contributors – tells our team: ""We have enough supplies to provide first aid. We can stop bleeding, clean and dress wounds, and remove superficial bullets. But patients with serious injuries to the chest, abdomen, head, or other vital organs need advanced hospitals."" Anwar adds: ""Even major hospitals would struggle if many such patients arrived at the same time."" Since 27 July, the clinic has treated around 50 to 60 critically injured patients, the doctor says, adding that he personally treated 15 to 20 with gunshot wounds. Protesters are scared to go to the main hospital because they fear they will be arrested or shot. The authorities hav","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 03:32:21 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5yvqk69enko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'What was my fault?': How India's exam paper leak ended young lives,'What was my fault?': How India's exam paper leak ended young lives,"'What was my fault?': How India's exam paper leak ended young lives Two families reveal the human toll of India's medical entrance examination paper  leak. Three days before he was due to sit India's rescheduled medical entrance examination, 17-year-old Kahaan Patel jumped from the sixth floor of his family's apartment building in the western city of Ahmedabad. When his family entered his bedroom after his death in the early hours of 18 June, it looked as though he might return at any moment. His laptop was still open. Text books, notebooks, pens and markers lay scattered across his bed and study table. He was wearing his headphones when he jumped. In one corner stood his electric guitar, plugged into an amplifier. On the shelves were Harry Potter novels, a book on Elon Musk, a collection of Goosebumps horror novels and board games. There was a trophy for ""consistent academic performance"". ""The only thing missing,"" his father, Prashant Patel, says quietly, ""was Kahaan."" Kahaan was one of at least 21 students who died by suicide, allegedly upset after this year's National Eligibility cum Entrance Test (NEET) was cancelled because of a question paper leak, forcing nearly two million candidates to take the examination again. Their deaths became the emotional core of anextraordinary youth uprisingthat spilled onto the streets of Delhi in July, where thousands demanded accountability for repeated exam failures. After days of protests led by the satiricalCockroach Janta Party(CJP), education minister Dharmendra Pradhan resigned. But behind the politics were families like Kahaan's – left trying to understand how an examination scandal ","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 23:35:54 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crl7jl1d95eo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,'Your legacy will live forever': Nepal mourns climber Nirmal Purja,'Your legacy will live forever': Nepal mourns climber Nirmal Purja,"'Your legacy will live forever': Nepal mourns climber Nirmal Purja Tributes are pouring out across Nepal after an avalanche killed some of the country's best mountaineers. Tributes are pouring out across Nepal after an avalanche killed all 10 members of an expedition on Pakistan's Broad Peak, six of whom were Nepalese. Among them wasNirmal Purja, also known as Nims,a British-Nepali climber most known for climbing the world's 14 tallest peaks in just over six months. His death has sent shockwaves through the global mountaineering community and through Nepal, where he is a celebrated figure for giving a spotlight to Nepali climbing guides. Nepal's Prime Minister Balendra Shah was among those who paid tribute, saying his ""history of courage, dedication and contribution will always remain inspiring"". In a moving tribute on Facebook, Purja's brother Kamal wrote: ""I feel as though I have lost my whole world. My brother was not only my younger brother - he was my heart."" ""Although our hearts are broken, we are incredibly proud of you. Your legacy will live forever,"" he added. At the Small Heaven School in Chitwan district, near Kathmandu, which Purja had once attended, students and staff lit candles and mourned his death. Sarbagya Paudel, one of the students at the vigil, said Purja's ""bravery has set a great example for our school. Not only has he made our school proud, but he has also brought pride to Nepal"". Purja is still fondly remembered by those who had known him from his schooling days.Shailendra Baral, his school hostel supervisor, told AFP that as a young boy Purja had been ""fearless and committed to achieving something"". ""He was honest and ","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 05:31:55 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c07rvy4e7mno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:13
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Solitude as a new aspirational lifestyle: 'We are not lonely',Solitude as a new aspirational lifestyle: 'We are not lonely',"Solitude as a new aspirational lifestyle: 'We are not lonely' A growing number of so-called ""loneliness influencers"" are spotlighting solo living without children or friends - and it's spurring debate. It's a Friday night. A young woman trudges back to her apartment after a day of work. She enters a spotless kitchen, unloads a bag of groceries, and unwraps a frozen pizza before sliding it in the oven. What follows is a nightly routine that is almost meditative in its mundanity: she lights a candle and gazes briefly at the snow-covered landscape outside her window, piles a premade salad onto a plate with the pizza, and settles on her sofa to eat in front of the TV. The tagline that accompanies the scene reads: ""POV you're a childfree and single girl that has no friends and lives alone so this is how you spend your Friday night."" At the centre of the video – which has amassed seven million views on TikTok – is Lana Isa, a software sales manager in her 20s based in Toronto. In addition to chronicling her nightly self-care routines, she takes her almost 400,000 followers across TikTok and Instagram on late-night fast food drives, reading sessions perched on a rock by the lake, even a saga of getting locked out of her apartment in the middle of the night. Isa is part of a growing, albeit niche corner of the internet where content creators, many of them women, share their daily rituals of living alone, choosing to spotlight a lifestyle that prioritises peace and solitude - the antithesis of social media excess. For Isa, unveiling the ""no friends"" aspect of her life was less of a tagline, but the ""reality"" she had been living for several years. After switching several high schools","Sat, 01 Aug 2026 23:56:19 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5y63dw2x3wo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Ginger hair portraits show unexpected 'golden thread' connecting Americans,Ginger hair portraits show unexpected 'golden thread' connecting Americans,"Ginger hair portraits show unexpected 'golden thread' connecting Americans For Kieran Dodds, photographing people with red hair has become something of an obsession over the past decade or more. For Kieran Dodds, photographing people with red hair has become something of an obsession over the past decade. The 45-year-old, from Edinburgh, who is himself a ""proud ginger"", began taking pictures in his homeland in 2013 as part of a series examining Scottish identity. His interest in the subject soon took him to other ginger hotspots such the Russian city of Perm and Jamaica's Treasure Beach. His2020 book Gingers spanned the worldbut after its release he noticed there was a ""huge gap"". ""I realised I didn't have any American citizens,"" Kieran told the BBC. ""Then all these people got in touch."" ""I was like 'oh wow, of course, the whole world is in the States',"" Kieran says. ""It's a microcosm."" He says a message from a Native American man who had red hair instantly aroused his curiosity. ""I found it very interesting and it made me think, 'where on earth did it come from?'"" The cover of his latest book -Gingers of America,external- features Joshua Elliot from the Muscogee (Creek) Nation in Oklahoma. He told Kieran that some of his mother's relatives were German and on his dad's side there were people from England and Sweden, so that could explain where his ginger hair comes from. ""My mom's side has always been involved with the Muscogee tribe and traditions and I've grown up around it,"" he said. Joshua knows he does not fit people's expectations of what a Native American should look like, but he said: ""Our chief's wife told me it's about your involvement in your community and","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 07:58:51 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ce97nyz4rvmo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,At the scene of deadly Moscow bomb blast,At the scene of deadly Moscow bomb blast,"At the scene of deadly Moscow bomb blast Three people have been killed and 21 injured after an explosive device detonated at an Italian restaurant, Russian media reports. At least three people have been killed and 21 injured after a homemade explosive device was detonated at an Italian restaurant in Moscow, Russian state media reports, quoting security officials. The blast happened at about 20:00 local time (17:00 GMT) on Kudrinskaya square near the city centre. The National Anti-Terrorist Committee said a woman carrying the explosives tried to enter the restaurant, which was holding a private event, but was stopped by a security guard. The BBC's Russia editor Steve Rosenberg reports from the scene in Moscow, where the explosion happened.","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:52:35 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/c1719pk0xq8o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Fastest runner to cross the Alps says it was an incredible experience,Fastest runner to cross the Alps says it was an incredible experience,"Fastest runner to cross the Alps says it was an incredible experience Sophie Woods ran the Via Alpina through storms, heat of up to 37C and dodged floods and wildfires across eight countries. Runner Sophie Woods became the fastest person to complete the 2,000km (1242-mile) Via Alpina ultramarathon beating both the men's and women's record. Woods ran the route through storms, heat of up to 37C and dodged floods and wildfires across eight countries. ""Through the whole thing, I was only ever thinking about the day, the hour, the sort of minute that I was in"", the runner told BBC Breakfast. The previous record was 35 days and Woods did it in just 29, crediting ice cream slushies for helping her power through.","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 10:59:55 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/crk5j6g8lmko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Wildfires scorch Washington state as thousands evacuated and emergency declared,Wildfires scorch Washington state as thousands evacuated and emergency declared,"Wildfires scorch Washington state as thousands evacuated and emergency declared State officials said wildfires across more than 250,000 acres have led to widespread evacuations and power outages. Wildfires across more than 250,000 acres have forced evacuations and caused power outages in the Washington state's Spokane area, US officials said. Washington State Governor Bob Ferguson declared a statewide emergency and burn ban on Saturday morning. ""Our state is facing a very serious situation with multiple fires, and our hearts go out to Washingtonians who are losing their homes"", Ferguson said. According to officials, no injuries or fatalities have been reported. Spokane is the state's second most populous city.","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 10:20:10 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cpd7912xyq4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Crowds line Amsterdam's canals for Pride boat parade,Crowds line Amsterdam's canals for Pride boat parade,"Crowds line Amsterdam's canals for Pride boat parade Costumed participants danced and waved from a colourful flotilla of 80 decorated vessels as they made their way through the city's waterways. Amsterdam came alive with music, colour and celebration on Saturday, as thousands of people flocked to the canals to watch the city's annual Pride boat parade. The Dutch capital is hosting this years' WorldPride event, drawing visitors from across the world. Costumed participants danced and waved from a colourful flotilla of 80 decorated vessels as they made their way through the city's waterways. Security was visibly heightened at the event, with police cruising the canals in motorboats. The measures come just a week after a deadly attack on a Pride event in Berlin, which killed one person and injured at least 29 others.","Sat, 01 Aug 2026 19:39:48 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cp3rqd9kp91o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
BBC News,Western Europe,en,Watch: Why Fifa dropped plans to sell stakes in the World Cup,Watch: Why Fifa dropped plans to sell stakes in the World Cup,"Watch: Why Fifa dropped plans to sell stakes in the World Cup The BBC's Shaimaa Khalil explains why Gianni Infantino has backed down on his controversial investment proposal. BBC's Shaimaa Khalil explains why Fifa president Gianni Infantino has backed down on his controversial investment proposal for the football governing body's major competitions. Infantino said it had become clear the project had ""created divisions"" that are ""no longer in the interest"" of its original objective. Read more on this storyhere.","Sat, 01 Aug 2026 02:44:09 GMT",https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cvgj0vldr1mo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss,2026-08-04 01:17:14
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"Dry Danube River threatens nuclear power in Romania, Hungary","Dry Danube River threatens nuclear power in Romania, Hungary","Dry Danube River threatens nuclear power in Romania, Hungary Hungary's Paks nuclear power plant was operating at about 10% of capacity because of a lack of cooling riverwater. In Romania, the military blew up a rock outcrop in a bid to improve water flow to the Cernavoda plant. Several central and eastern European countries are facing power shortages and impacts on industry amid prolonged hot and dry weather andrecord low levels on the Danube Riverand other waterways. Hungarynarrowly averted switching off its lone nuclear plant, but had still powered it down to a fraction of capacity. InRomania, the navy demolished a rocky outcrop exposed in the Danube's riverbed, trying to improve the water flow. And inSerbia, hydroelectric power output was severely impacted by the dry conditions. Continued hot and dry weather is forecast during the week, providing little sign of relief. Romania's military detonated a rocky outcrop exposed by the low water levels on a branch of the Danube, seeking to improve the flow of cooling water to the Cernavoda nuclear plant, which typically supplies about one-fifth of the country's power. The plant draws all of its cooling water from the Danube. Soldiers detonated a rock some 50 kilometers (roughly 30 miles) upstream. The debris would be taken to a strategic spot and sunk to help raise the water level. Defense Minister Radu Miruta said the operation was a success, and also said the military had been waiting for a chance to access and demolish the rocks for decades. If the partial diversion of the water fails, Miruta said, the plant would likely have to shut down entirely by Thursday. Caretaker Prime Minister Ilie Bolojan said that carmakers Dacia and Ford had agreed to suspend production in Romania until August 19, following",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/dry-danube-river-threatens-nuclear-power-in-romania-hungary/a-78223402?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:16
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Ceuta: EU calls borders a 'shared European responsibility',Ceuta: EU calls borders a 'shared European responsibility',"Ceuta: EU calls borders a 'shared European responsibility' The European Commission has offered Morocco more support at the border to Ceuta and Melilla, and called for ""united action"" from the bloc on border security. Its interior ministers will discuss the issue on Tuesday. European CommissionPresidentUrsula von der Leyenhas toldSpain's prime minister that border security is a ""shared European responsibility"" and suggested Brussels could provide more assistance toMorocco. This comes as Spain tries to return the last oftens of thousands of people who swam around or otherwise breached the borderto the North African exclave of Ceuta last week. European Union interior ministers will convene for an emergency video meeting on Tueday to discuss potential responses. The scenes had led to friction betwen EU members and to Italy and France implementing temporary border controls, even though it's not possible to travel from Ceuta to the mainland without providing identification. Von der Leyen wrote to Prime MinisterPedro Sanchezon Monday, which was soon seen by several news agencies, calling for ""united action"" on border security within the bloc. ""From this incident it is clear that we must do more to further strengthen our borders at critical points,"" she wrote. ""The protection of all our external borders is a shared European responsibility,"" von der Leyen said, calling for vigilant monitoring and the use of physical barriers where needed. She commended Sanchez on his ""swift handling"" of the situation, withthe vast majority of the roughly 50,000 people having already returned across the border to Morocco. Ceuta's top government official Juan Jesus Vivas estimated that between 3,000 and 5,000 people remained in the city, with the Spanish governemnt's deleg",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/ceuta-eu-calls-borders-a-shared-european-responsibility/a-78220701?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:16
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Can PM Modi reconnect with India's Gen Z?,Can PM Modi reconnect with India's Gen Z?,"Can PM Modi reconnect with India's Gen Z? Student protests exposed a growing disconnect between PM Narendra Modi's BJP and young Indians. Experts say Instagram reels alone won't close it. As students filled India's streets todemand accountability from the government, Indian Prime MinisterNarendra Modiseemed to have realized that he needed to connect more effectively with the country's youth. Modi posted on Instagram a rare selfie video in which he said he was pained by their suffering and promised remedial steps. But it was not enough to satisfy the protesters' concerns, and five Gen Z-style reels later, that gap seems as wide as ever. ""We ain't friends,"" one comment under the first reel read. It echoed thousands of others reacting to a midnight video drop from the PM. In one of Modi's lesser-produced videos, he addressed the youth as ""friends"" before finally speaking about the exam at the center of the protests. It was the prime minister’s response to the biggest political crisis he has faced in his third term so far, over acancelled high-stakes medical entrance examwhich affected millions of aspirants. Since the first video, he's improved camera angles and lighting, tried varied backgrounds, and even started and ended the videos himself like a one-man content creator. The response remains tepid. For Modi's ruling Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP), theprotestscame at the peak of its power, but the unrest has dimmed that shine, and the disconnect with the youth is substantial, experts told DW. In 2024, the average age of MPs elected to India's Parliament reached a record 56 years, which is a problem, said Shipra Baduni, CEO of Young Leaders for Active Citizenship, ",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/can-pm-modi-reconnect-with-india-s-gen-z/a-78216627?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:18
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Russia: Bridge-building with North Korea,Russia: Bridge-building with North Korea,"Russia: Bridge-building with North Korea In the far east, well away from world events, Russia and North Korea are building their first road connection. The project has set alarm bells ringing in Ukraine. For years, Khasan was little more than a very remote place on the edge ofRussia. Less than 500 people live in the village in the country's far east, more than 5,600 miles (9,012 kilometers) from Moscow. But it's here that Russia shares a mere 10.5-mile border withNorth Korea— and Khasan is the only Russian settlement along that short stretch. Since 1959, the so-called Korean-Russian Friendship Railway Bridge has crossed the Tumen River here, serving as the only land connection between the two countries. But in recent years, Russia and North Korea have been building a parallel road bridge just a few hundred yards away. The bridge is now nearing completion ahead of schedule, raising concerns in Kyiv that Russia could use it to secretly bring North Korean troops to the front lines inUkraine. Since the start ofRussia's invasion of Ukrainein 2022, Moscow and Pyongyang haveimproved their ties. Both countries face Western sanctions and are diplomatically largely isolated. In June 2024, Russian President Vladimir Putin and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un signed a strategic partnership deal which in part focused on expanding military cooperation. That meant large-scale weapons shipments as well as thedeployment of North Korean soldiersto support Russia's war in Ukraine. At the same time, both governments agreed to build the new road bridge. ""The opening of the new bridge will boost bilateral trade, optimize logistics, strengthen cultural ties and transform border regions into dynamica",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/russia-bridge-building-with-north-korea/a-78212053?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:18
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Cuba suffers yet another nationwide blackout,Cuba suffers yet another nationwide blackout,"Cuba suffers yet another nationwide blackout Cuba's electricity grid has collapsed again after three nationwide blackouts in July. The power failure comes amid a US-imposed oil blockade on the communist island. Cuba's state-owned grid operator said the island's power system collapsed late on Sunday, the latest in aseries of rolling power cutscaused by aging infrastructure and aUS energy embargo. The outage comes on the heels of three nationwide power outages last month. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video The state-run utility UNE said there was a ""total disconnection"" of the national electricity system, which means the entire country of 10 million people, including the capital, Havana, has been plunged into darkness. The Ministry of Energy said that protocols to reactivate the grid were underway. The series of outages afflicting the island in recent times have also affected water supplies and cooling systems, making normal life extremely difficult. Cuba, which relies largely on aging oil-fired power plants, lost access to fuel from its previous primary supplier, Venezuela, after US PresidentDonald Trumpimposed anoil blockadefollowingWashington's ouster and capture of Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro on January 3. Imports fromMexico, which subsequently became Cuba's key supplier of oil, have also ceased under US pressure. Supplies brought by asingle Russian tanker in March with Washington's authorizationhave been exhausted. Cuba has also been under a broader US embargo for decades. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video Cuba is also faci",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/cuba-suffers-yet-another-nationwide-blackout/a-78210761?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:19
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Man arrested over death of British woman found in suitcase in Athens,Man arrested over death of British woman found in suitcase in Athens,"Man arrested over death of British woman found in suitcase in Athens The body of Scottish national Elisabeth-Jane Ross was discovered last month in a suitcase in a derelict building in Athens. A 26-year-old man has been arrested by Greek police in connection with the death of a British woman whose body was found in a suitcase inAthenslast month. ""Officers of the Directorate for Combating Organized Crime have solved the homicide of a 38-year-oldUKnational, whose body was found inside a suitcase in an abandoned building in the Kypseli area on July 18,"" the Hellenic Police said on Sunday. The victim, identified in British and Greek media reports as 38-year-old Elisabeth-Jane Ross from Scotland, arrived inGreecein late June and was last known to be in the Athens area on July 10. She was later reported missing. Police said the suspect, a foreign national, confessed on Thursday and now faces charges including intentional homicide, robbery and firearms-related offenses. ""It emerged that the [suspect] placed the body in a suitcase and transported it to the abandoned premises. Furthermore, in the days that followed, the 26-year-old used the victim's bank cards to withdraw sums of money,"" police said. According to reports, the suspect said the victim was already dead when he found her, although police made no mention of this in their statement. A replica pistol and a knife were recovered from the suspect's home. Greek police said international cooperation, including support from Interpol authorities in the US and UK law enforcement agencies, helped identify the victim despite advanced decomposition. Edited by: Roshni Majumdar Don't let the algorithm hide the news. If you rel",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/man-arrested-over-death-of-british-woman-found-in-suitcase-in-athens/a-78210183?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:20
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"Small insects, big damage: The global ant trade is booming","Small insects, big damage: The global ant trade is booming","Small insects, big damage: The global ant trade is booming Legal or not, the trade in African ants bound for Europe is booming. What do hobbyists find appealing about these insects, and what risks does the trade pose to ecosystems, including power outages? They are unusual pieces of evidence that regularly appear inKenyancourtrooms: thousands of ants packed into tiny containers. Since 2025, such finds have been presented in court several times. Staff from the Kenya Wildlife Service discovered them during baggage inspections. In multiple cases, they identified thousands of specimens of the sought-after Messor Cephalotes species also known as theGiant African Harvester Ant. A single queen can fetch more than €200 ($230) on the market. The export of wild animals is generally prohibited in Kenya. Due to high levels of poaching — particularly involving elephants and rhinoceroses — many African countries have introduced tough laws against wildlife trafficking. Kenya has also tightened its legislation. Under these rules, aChinesenational who attempted to export ants was sentenced last April to one year in prison for biopiracy. Given thegrowing scale of smuggling and its ecological consequences, the judge said the sentence should serve as a deterrent. It was already the second major ant-smuggling case in 12 months. The previous year, the wildlife service stopped four men who referred to themselves as the ""Ant Gang."" Among them were two 19-year-oldBelgianswho were trying to smuggle 5,500 queens to Europe. They were fined around €5,000 each. Whenever such cases arise, authorities often call entomologist Dino Martins to the airport. ""I was shocked,"" he said. ""Not only by the sheer number of ants, but also because they were queen ants."" Th",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/small-insects-big-damage-the-global-ant-trade-is-booming/a-78186729?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:20
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,"Moscow restaurant blast a 'brutal terror attack,' mayor says","Moscow restaurant blast a 'brutal terror attack,' mayor says","Moscow restaurant blast a 'brutal terror attack,' mayor says The deadly explosion outside an upscale Moscow restaurant at Kudrinskaya Square was a ""brutal terror attack,"" Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin said, promising the perpetrators would be punished. But many questions remain. An explosion at a restaurant in centralMoscowkilled at least three people, including the suspected bomber on Saturday night, authorities said. Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin described it as a ""brutal terrorist attack."" ""Law enforcement agencies are investigating the circumstances of the incident. Those responsible for this crime will certainly be found and face the punishment they deserve,"" he said in an online post on Sunday. Russia'sAnti-Terrorism Committee said a woman carrying ""a homemade explosive device"" tried to enter the restaurant on Kudrinskaya Square but was stopped by a security guard. The device exploded at the entrance, killing the woman, the guard and a restaurant patron. State news agency RIA Novosti reported that at least 21 other people were injured in the blast, which occurred shortly before 8 p.m. local time (5 p.m. UTC). The restaurant's website said the venue had been closed on Saturday for a private event. Heavily armed law enforcement officers sealed off the area and launched an investigation. Authorities did not immediately identify those they believed to be behind the attack. Russia has faced several attacks since the start of thewar in Ukrainein 2022. They blamed a ‌series of assassinations and attempted killings on Kyiv. Russian officials have yet to comment on any possible Ukrainian involvement into the Kudrinskaya Square blast. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video However,Ukra",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/moscow-restaurant-blast-a-brutal-terror-attack-mayor-says/a-78205022?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:20
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Poland: Prime Minister Tusk defies anti-Ukrainian violence,Poland: Prime Minister Tusk defies anti-Ukrainian violence,"Poland: Prime Minister Tusk defies anti-Ukrainian violence Anti-Ukrainian sentiment in Poland is on the rise, with numerous attacks against refugees from the war in Ukraine. Prime Minister Donald Tusk has spoken out against the violence and warned of the danger from Russia. The Polish leader didn't mince his words. ""We must wage a resolute fight against this violence, because thugs, hooligans, and this culture of violence are becoming increasingly brazen and insolent — and also because they have received political support,""Donald Tusksaid on Tuesday, before a Polish cabinet meeting in Warsaw. The liberal-conservative prime minister said he deplored the fact that ""more and more such incidents"" are taking place all overPoland. Tusk's comments came in response to an incident in Wroclaw two days earlier, in which three Polish men brutally beat up a young Ukrainian man and his girlfriend on the street. Cellphone videos show the attackers kicking the Ukrainian man as he lies on the ground. The woman was also hit, sustaining injuries to her neck and ear. The attack came after an argument in a shop; the attackers took exception to the couple's Ukrainian accents. Polish police acted swiftly: Two of the attackers, both of whom have previous convictions, are already in custody, and a third is being sought. Tusk warned that such incidents negatively impact Poland's image abroad. The country's reputation was greatly enhanced by itswillingness to provide assistanceafter the start ofRussia's full-scale war against Ukraine. This could be destroyed, said Tusk, ""if we allow thugs, hooligans and common criminals to beat and humiliate people just because they have a slightly different accent."" Poland provided military and humanitarian assistance to its neighbor fro",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/poland-prime-minister-tusk-defies-anti-ukrainian-violence/a-78203187?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:21
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,On the front line of Europe's battle against wildfires,On the front line of Europe's battle against wildfires,"On the front line of Europe's battle against wildfires French firefighters have won out over a massive blaze, with almost 200,000 evacuees returning home. But Europe's climate-fueled wildfire season is far from over. The signal to move comes suddenly — and within a minute or so the fire truck is full and speeding off into the distance, a cloud of orange dust swirling in its wake. ""There's always a bit of adrenaline when you get the call,"" marine firefighter Julien says. ""But we're trained; we're prepared."" Maitre Julien — whom DW can only identity by rank and first name — is part of Marseille-based battalion deployed to France's Gironde region to tackle a wildfire which promptedthe largest evacuations in the countrysince World War II. Some 264 firefighters were injured in the fight against the Gironde fire, four of whom lost their lives. In nearby towns, French flags still fly at half mast to mark the loss. ""It's always on our mind,"" Lieutenant Damien, another marine firefighter, explains as his team members haul the firehose out behind him. Letitia, a firefighter and medic, said she tries to ask how colleagues are feeling ""without being too intrusive."" ""It's the fatigue above all,"" she says when asked about the most common problem affecting her colleagues. ""They're sleeping very little."" ""Do you see all all of that?"" Maitre Julien asks, gesturing out the car window as he drives past rows of blackened trees and swathes of scorched earth at the roadside. ""All of that's already burned. But you can see small peels of smoke here and there. We absolutely have to extinguish those so fires don't restart."" When Julien pulls up at another hotspot, the risk of flare-ups comes sharply into focus: Th",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/on-the-front-line-of-europe-s-battle-against-wildfires/a-78204324?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:21
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,Hungary to shut sole nuclear plant for first time on Sunday,Hungary to shut sole nuclear plant for first time on Sunday,"Hungary to shut sole nuclear plant for first time on Sunday Record-low water levels on the Danube forced the Paks plant offline, leaving Hungary facing a potential power crunch. In neighboring Serbia, energy production at two hydroelectric plants has also been reduced. Hungarywill shut down the Paks nuclear power plant on Sunday for the first time in its 44-year history, Prime Minister Peter Magyar said. ""We are facing the most critical five days ahead,"" Magyar said. ""Tomorrow, ​the Paks power plant will not be generating, while the hottest days are ahead."" He made the announcement on social media late Saturday night, saying the decision was taken after afurther drop in the Danube's water level. The plant, located 120 kilometers (74.5 miles) south of the capital, Budapest, uses water from the Danube to cool its reactors. It provides more than 40% of Hungary's electricity generation. ""The power grid, our public services and ourselves will all come under enormous ⁠strain,"" ​said Magyar, who has warned that the Paks plant could remain idle ​for weeks. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsupports HTML5 video The Danube has fallen to record-low levels in some areas after months ofbelow-average rainfallandsuccessive heat wavessince May. Hungary's water authority projected that the Danube, which flows fromGermanyto theBlack Sea, would fall further in the coming days. It measured the Danube's water level in Budapest at 23 centimeters (9 inches) on Wednesday morning, well below the previous record low of 33 centimeters (13 inches) set in 2018. As a result, output at the Paks nuclear power plant had already been cut earlier in the week and some reactors shut down. ""Due to a further decline in the D",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/hungary-to-shut-sole-nuclear-plant-for-first-time-on-sunday/a-78205668?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:22
Deutsche Welle (EN),Western Europe,en,How does eastern Europe commemorate the Roma Holocaust?,How does eastern Europe commemorate the Roma Holocaust?,"How does eastern Europe commemorate the Roma Holocaust? In 2015, the EU designated August 2 as the European Roma Holocaust Memorial Day. In Germany, the day is marked with memorial services, educational activites and public addresses. And in eastern Europe? It took decades beforeGermanyrecognized the Romani Holocaust — the Nazigenocideof 500,000 European Roma and Sinti duringWorld War II. Several more decades passed before the European Holocaust Memorial Day for Sinti and Roma became an official German and European day of remembrance, celebrated each year on August 2. Based on initiatives by Polish Roma groups, the Nazi liquidation of the so-called ""Gypsy Family Camp"" in theAuschwitz-Birkenau Concentration and Extermination Campon the evening of August 2-3, 1944, was first commemorated half a century later, in 1994. Thousands of deported Roma and Sinti had already been killed as a result of malnutrition and disease. That evening, thousands more were forced into gas chambers, shot and beaten to death by Nazi henchmen. In 2015, the European Parliament declared this date the European Roma Holocaust Memorial Day. In Germany and other European countries, the genocide is commemorated by officials with the participation of government representatives. In some cases, representatives of European governments travel to the memorial site in Auschwitz. In most cases, however, countries hold their own memorial services — particularly in central and southeastern Europe, where Nazi extermination commonly targetedRomani peoples. These days, such memorial days also take the opportunity to raise awareness for ongoing Antiziganism and the consequences it has. To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser thatsu",No Date,https://www.dw.com/en/how-does-eastern-europe-commemorate-the-roma-holocaust/a-78183503?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf,2026-08-04 01:17:23
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"US northwest wildfires force 65,000 to flee as hundreds of buildings burn in Spokane","US northwest wildfires force 65,000 to flee as hundreds of buildings burn in Spokane","US northwest wildfires force 65,000 to flee as hundreds of buildings burn in Spokane Firefighters made progress against several blazes Monday after wildfires driven by drought, extreme heat and strong winds tore through Washington state's second-largest city, destroying up to 1,100 structures and prompting what officials called the worst natural disaster in Spokane's history. Firefighters made progress against several blazes Monday after wildfires driven by drought, extreme heat and strong winds tore through Washington state's second-largest city, destroying up to 1,100 structures and prompting what officials called the worst natural disaster in Spokane's history. Issued on:04/08/2026 - 02:40Modified:04/08/2026 - 02:45 To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Wildfires supercharged by drought and intense heattore through the northwestern US city of Spokane, forcing the evacuation of 65,000 people and reducing hundreds of buildings to cinders asfirefightersraced Monday to contain the flames. Aerial flybys revealed somewhere between 700-1,110 structures have been lost since the fires broke out over the weekend, authorities said. Anxious families awaited news about when they could return. Three fires have combined to burn 8,026 acres (3,248 hectares) in the Spokane area of Washington state according to InciWeb, a federal interagency information system, with firefighters making use of cooler temperatures, higher humidities and thick smoke to make ""significant gains"" against two of them. Local officials said so far no one has been reported hurt. Cooler temperatures were expected Monday and Tuesday, though the respite will be brief, with a warming ","Tue, 04 Aug 2026 00:40:29 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20260804-us-northwest-wildfires-force-65-000-to-flee-as-hundreds-of-buildings-burn-in-spokane,2026-08-04 01:17:23
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"Trump says US-Iran talks continue, warns Tehran faces 'last chance'","Trump says US-Iran talks continue, warns Tehran faces 'last chance'","Trump says US-Iran talks continue, warns Tehran faces 'last chance' The US president insisted negotiations were underway despite Tehran's denials, saying Iran had a final opportunity to reach an agreement on the Strait of Hormuz and its nuclear programme before facing what he called 'decapitation.' The US president insisted negotiations were underway despite Tehran's denials, saying Iran had a final opportunity to reach an agreement on the Strait of Hormuz and its nuclear programme before facing what he called 'decapitation.' Issued on:04/08/2026 - 00:57 USPresidentDonald Trumpsaid Monday that negotiations withIranwere ongoing despite Tehran's denials and the Islamic Republic was facing a ""last chance before decapitation."" Trump, speaking to reporters at theWhite House, said he agreed to continue to pursue talks with Iran at the request ofSaudi Arabia, theUnited Arab Emirates,Qatarand other countries. Iran's foreign ministry denied on Monday that negotiations with Washington were taking place, but Trump insisted they were. ""We are talking right now,"" he said. ""This is a last chance for them to sign a good document,"" the US president added. ""I want to give them every last chance before decapitation."" The talks are focused on reopening theStrait of Hormuz, which Trump said could come ""literally by tomorrow,"" and the denuclearization of Iran, which he said could ""take a little while."" Washington and Tehran have been at war since February 28, when the United States andIsraellaunched surprise strikes against Iran, though months of on-and-off diplomacy have led to periods of relative calm. More than five months later, Iran remains able to fire missiles anddronesat US and allied targets in the region and retains its enricheduraniumstockpiles. In what has become a familiar patt","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 22:57:33 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20260803-trump-says-us-iran-talks-continue-warns-tehran-faces-last-chance,2026-08-04 01:17:23
