France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,"Amid a drought and soaring temperatures, Hungary shuts down its nuclear plant","Amid a drought and soaring temperatures, Hungary shuts down its nuclear plant","Amid a drought and soaring temperatures, Hungary shuts down its nuclear plant Nuclear power is often touted as a solution to global warming. It comes with risks, but creates energy without the same greenhouse gas emissions as coal or gas plants. However, rising temperatures and drought in Europe are creating problems and shutdowns at nuclear power plants. With water levels too low to cool the reactors, Hungary - for example - is shutting down the reactor that's meant to provide energy for half the country. To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Issued on:03/08/2026 - 14:34 Nuclear power is often touted as a solution to global warming. It comes with risks, but creates energy without the same greenhouse gas emissions as coal or gas plants. However, rising temperatures and drought in Europe are creating problems and shutdowns at nuclear power plants. With water levels too low to cool the reactors, Hungary - for example - is shutting down the reactor that's meant to provide energy for half the country. The content you requested does not exist or is not available anymore.","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:34:47 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/amid-a-drought-and-soaring-temperatures-hungary-shuts-down-its-nuclear-plant,2026-08-04 01:17:25
France 24 (EN),Western Europe,en,Ceuta migrant crisis:  Far-right govts 'ridiculously using these images' to stoke fear for votes,Ceuta migrant crisis:  Far-right govts 'ridiculously using these images' to stoke fear for votes,"Ceuta migrant crisis:  Far-right govts 'ridiculously using these images' to stoke fear for votes Jennie Shin is pleased to welcome Antoni Vives, policy analyst and the former Deputy Mayor of Barcelona. Vives offers a nuanced geopolitical interpretation of the Ceuta migration crisis, arguing that the events cannot be understood solely as a humanitarian emergency or a spontaneous wave of migration. Vives frames the crisis within a broader framework encompassing historical colonial legacies, demographic and economic inequalities, regional power competition between Morocco and Algeria, and the strategic use of migration as an instrument of statecraft. While acknowledging the aspirations of young Moroccans seeking economic opportunity, he contends that the scale and timing of the crossings reflect deliberate geopolitical signalling by Morocco toward Spain. To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Issued on:03/08/2026 - 14:20 Jennie Shin is pleased to welcome Antoni Vives, policy analyst and the former Deputy Mayor of Barcelona. Vives offers a nuanced geopolitical interpretation of the Ceuta migration crisis, arguing that the events cannot be understood solely as a humanitarian emergency or a spontaneous wave of migration. Vives frames the crisis within a broader framework encompassing historical colonial legacies, demographic and economic inequalities, regional power competition between Morocco and Algeria, and the strategic use of migration as an instrument of statecraft. While acknowledging the aspirations of young Moroccans seeking economic opportunity, he contends that the scale and timing of the crossings reflect deliberate geopolitical signalling by Morocco toward Spain. The content you requested does not exist or is not available anymore.","Mon, 03 Aug 2026 12:20:57 GMT",https://www.france24.com/en/ceuta-migrant-crisis-far-right-govts-ridiculously-using-these-images-to-stoke-fear-for-votes,2026-08-04 01:17:25
