The Hindu,South Asia,en,Veteran mountaineer Purja’s body found three days after avalanche on Broad Peak in PoK,Veteran mountaineer Purja’s body found three days after avalanche on Broad Peak in PoK,"Veteran mountaineer Purja’s body found three days after avalanche on Broad Peak in PoK The ground rescue team has reached Nirmal Purja ‘Nims Dai’ at approximately 5,700m on Broad Peak, where three additional bodies have also been sighted, though their identities remain unconfirmed, said Alpine Club of Pakistan You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- August 02, 2026 05:11 pm IST - Lahore Nirmal Purja. File | Photo Credit: AP The body of veteran Nepalese-British mountaineer Nirmal Purja was found on Sunday (August 2, 2026), three days after he and nine fellow climbers were swept away in an avalanche on Broad Peak, located in Pakistan-occupied Kashmir’s Gilgit-Baltistan. Also read |Who is Nirmal Purja, renowned Nepalese climber killed in Pakistan avalanche? The Alpine Club of Pakistan (ACP) said the 43-year-old mountaineer’s body was found at an altitude of around 5,700 metres. So far, the bodies of five climbers have been recovered, and efforts are underway to locate the remaining five, it said. ""The ground rescue team has reached Nirmal Purja ‘Nims Dai’ at approximately 5,700m on Broad Peak, where three additional bodies have also been sighted, though their identities remain unconfirmed,” the ACP said. The ground team will bring the bodies down to the Japanese camp, it said, adding that recovery efforts continue under extremely difficult terrain conditions. The ACP said the mountain’s terrain, risk of avalanches and steep slopes ar","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 17:11:50 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/veteran-mountaineer-purjas-body-found-three-days-after-avalanche-on-broad-peak-in-pok/article71297621.ece,2026-08-04 01:21:09
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Iran media denies Trump claim that Tehran asked U.S. not to strike,Iran media denies Trump claim that Tehran asked U.S. not to strike,"Iran media denies Trump claim that Tehran asked U.S. not to strike On August 1, Donald Trump said the U.S. was “ready to go against the Islamic republic” but that Tehran and other West Asian countries had asked him to hold off on fresh strikes You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- August 02, 2026 04:44 pm IST - Tehran, Iran U.S. President Donald Trump. File. | Photo Credit: Reuters Iran’sMehrnews agency on Sunday (August 2, 2026) deniedPresident Donald Trump’s claim that the Islamic republic asked the United States to refrain from conducting new strikes. Citing military officials, the agency said the allegation “was nothing but a new lie” and that the Iranian armed forces were “on high alert and ready for any eventuality”. On Saturday (August 1, 2026), Mr. Trump said the U.S. was “ready to go against the Islamic republic” but that Tehran and other West Asian countries had asked him to hold off on fresh strikes. He said he had made the decision, subject to reaching a deal with Iran. “This would include the Immediate, Complete and Total OPENING OF THE HORMUZ STRAIT, and an end to Iran’s nuclear threat,” wrote Mr. Trump in a Truth Social post. On Sunday (August 2, 2026), Iran’sFarsnews agency, citing a source close to Iranian negotiators, said “as long as the United States maintains its hostile actions, the Strait of Hormuz will remain closed”. Iran has blockaded H","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:44:16 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-media-denies-trump-claim-that-tehran-asked-us-not-to-strike/article71297512.ece,2026-08-04 01:21:10
The Hindu,South Asia,en,Sri Lanka deploys troops after prison riot,Sri Lanka deploys troops after prison riot,"Sri Lanka deploys troops after prison riot The inmates at Mahara Prison had completely destroyed the kitchen, the hospital, a newly built rehabilitation building and vocational training facilities You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- August 02, 2026 03:53 pm IST - Colombo, Sri Lanka A general view shows a damaged building after a riot at the Mahara Prison complex on the outskirts of Colombo on August 2, 2026. | Photo Credit: AFP Sri Lanka deployed hundreds of troops and elite commandos on Sunday (August 2, 2026) to containriots at an overcrowded prisonin which one inmate was killed and six others wounded, police said. Deputy police inspector-general Sanjeewa Medawatte said the situation was under control after inmates destroyed multiple buildings on Saturday (August 1, 2026) inside Mahara Prison, just outside the capital Colombo. “We got reinforcements from the armed forces and the Special Task Force commandos to restore order and send the inmates back to their cells,” Mr. Medawatte told reporters outside the prison. In November 2020, a riot erupted at the facility in which 11 inmates were shot and killed, and nearly 120 others wounded. The latest unrest came almost a month afteranother prison riot in the same region, in which 10 guards and 21 inmates were killed. Mr. Medawatte said inmates at Mahara Prison had com","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 15:53:13 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/sri-lanka-deploys-troops-after-prison-riot/article71297438.ece,2026-08-04 01:21:10
The Hindu,South Asia,en,"Sarnath earns UNESCO World Heritage tag, takes India’s tally to 45","Sarnath earns UNESCO World Heritage tag, takes India’s tally to 45","Sarnath earns UNESCO World Heritage tag, takes India’s tally to 45 You are logged in Loading... You don’t have any Active Subscription. Subscribed with another email? Logout and Login with that one. Your active subscription(s) Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription Products you've access to Additional Subscription Benefits Account Settings Need help with your subscription? Updated- August 02, 2026 01:53 pm IST - HYDERABAD A Buddhist devout reads in front of Dhamek Stupa in the ancient Buddhist site of Sarnath. | Photo Credit: Representational Photo The ancient Buddhist site of Sarnath in Varanasi was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List during the 48th session of the World Heritage Committee, held in Busan, South Korea. The announcement was made by the committee’s chairperson, Lee Byong Hyun, at the annual conclave on Sunday. Sarnath recognised as UNESCO World Heritage Site: Full list of India's sites, criteria for inclusion With the inscription of Sarnath, India now has 45 UNESCO World Heritage Sites. The Uttar Pradesh site has been recognised as a serial property comprising the Chaukhandi Stupa and the Archaeological Remains of Sarnath, which include the preserved archaeological and architectural remains of the ancient Buddhist site. Sarnath is believed to be the place where Gautama Buddha delivered his first sermon after attaining enlightenment in the 6th century BCE. Also known as Rishipatana, Ishipatana, Mrigadava or the Deer Park, it is regarded as t","Sun, 02 Aug 2026 13:53:52 +0530",https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/sarnath-earns-unesco-world-heritage-tag-takes-indias-tally-to-45/article71266825.ece,2026-08-04 01:21:10
