45 US states, with both victims having underlying health cond","Tue, 4 Aug 2026 06:33:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/globe-at-a-glance-from-drone-strike-on-busy-russian-beach-to-25-states-suing-trump-14035728.html,2026-08-04 01:22:01
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"At least 22 killed, including civilians, children, as Russia and Ukraine exchange attacks","At least 22 killed, including civilians, children, as Russia and Ukraine exchange attacks","At least 22 killed, including civilians, children, as Russia and Ukraine exchange attacks At least 22 people were killed in fresh attacks across Russia and Ukraine as both sides reported civilian casualties and continued military operations At least 22 people were killed in fresh attacks across Russia and Ukraine as both sides reported civilian casualties and continued military operations At least 22 people were killed in the latest wave of attacks across Russia and Ukraine on Monday, officials from both countries said, with civilian casualties reported on both sides. Russia reported deaths in Krasnodar, Crimea and Belgorod, while Ukraine said strikes in the Dnipropetrovsk and Zaporizhzhia regions killed civilians, including children. The latest exchanges come amid continued fighting on multiple fronts, with Russia also claiming territorial gains in eastern Ukraine and Kyiv seeking greater military support from the US. Veniamin Kondratyev, governor of Russia's southern Krasnodar region, said seven people, including three children, were killed and nearly 40 others were wounded after drone debris fell on the resort village of Arkhipo-Osipovka near Gelendzhik. His statement on Telegram followed an update from Sergey Aksyonov, the Moscow-appointed head of Russian-occupied Crimea, who said the death toll there had risen to four, while two others were injured. Acting Belgorod Governor Alexander Shuvaev reported four civilian deaths and 17 injuries in settlements near the border. In Ukraine, three people, including an emergency response worker and his eight-year-old son, were killed in a Russian strike on a petrol station in the Kryvyi Rih district of the Dnipropetrovsk region. Regional Governor Oleksandr Hanzha said i","Tue, 4 Aug 2026 06:25:10 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/at-least-22-killed-including-civilians-children-as-russia-and-ukraine-exchange-attacks-14035730.html,2026-08-04 01:22:01
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Trump's peace board reassures Netanyahu, says Gaza withdrawal will begin only after Hamas disarms","Trump's peace board reassures Netanyahu, says Gaza withdrawal will begin only after Hamas disarms","Trump's peace board reassures Netanyahu, says Gaza withdrawal will begin only after Hamas disarms Trump's Board of Peace has assured Benjamin Netanyahu that Israel's withdrawal from Gaza will begin only after Hamas completes its disarmament, while renewed strikes and diplomatic differences continue Trump's Board of Peace has assured Benjamin Netanyahu that Israel's withdrawal from Gaza will begin only after Hamas completes its disarmament, while renewed strikes and diplomatic differences continue Israel's withdrawal from Gaza will begin only after Hamas has fully disarmed, US President Donald Trump's Board of Peace said on Monday, offering assurances to Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu after Israel objected to the proposed framework. The clarification followed concerns raised by Netanyahu's government over the board's earlier proposal calling for a phased Israeli withdrawal, prompting two members of the security cabinet to seek a fresh vote to scrap the Trump-backed plan. The board's statement came after Nickolay Mladenov, the Board of Peace's high representative for Gaza, met Netanyahu in talks he described as difficult. Following the meeting, the board said it had reached a ""common understanding of the shared objectives"" with Israel. According to the board, Israel's withdrawal beyond the Yellow Line, which marks the area where Hamas still retains control, ""will take place only once decommissioning (of weapons) is complete, as Hamas committed to the mediators"". The board added that even after the withdrawal begins, Israel would continue to maintain control over large parts of Gaza under the Trump-led October ceasefire framework. Netanyahu's office said it had conveyed its concerns to the US after Trump described Hamas's agreement to hand over its weapons to a nascent ","Tue, 4 Aug 2026 06:32:06 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trumps-peace-board-reassures-netanyahu-says-gaza-withdrawal-will-begin-only-after-hamas-disarms-14035725.html,2026-08-04 01:22:02
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,25 Democratic-led US states sue to block Trump's tariffs on India and 59 other trading partners,25 Democratic-led US states sue to block Trump's tariffs on India and 59 other trading partners,"25 Democratic-led US states sue to block Trump's tariffs on India and 59 other trading partners A coalition of 25 Democratic-led US states has sued the Trump administration to block tariffs on India and 59 other trading partners, arguing the import duties are unlawful and an attempt to bypass earlier court rulings against the president's trade policy A coalition of 25 Democratic-led US states has sued the Trump administration to block tariffs on India and 59 other trading partners, arguing the import duties are unlawful and an attempt to bypass earlier court rulings against the president's trade policy President Donald Trump's new tariff regime has landed back in court after 25 Democratic-led US states filed a lawsuit seeking to overturn duties imposed on India and 59 other trading partners, calling the measures an illegal attempt to bypass previous court rulings. The lawsuit, filed in the US Court of International Trade in New York, asks the court to declare the tariffs unlawful, block their enforcement and order the Trump administration to refund duties already collected. The states argue that the latest levies are effectively a fresh version of sweeping import taxes that US courts, including the Supreme Court, have already ruled the president cannot impose unilaterally. The legal challenge is the latest in a series of courtroom battles over Trump's trade policy, underscoring the administration's determination to preserve its tariff strategy by relying on different provisions of US trade law. The tariffs, which took effect in July, impose duties of 10 per cent and 12.5 per cent on imports from 60 trading partners, including India, China, Canada, Japan, Taiwan, Norway and the European Union. The Trump administration has justified the tariffs under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, arguing that the affected countries ","Tue, 4 Aug 2026 06:26:35 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/business/trump-tariffs-india-us-states-lawsuit-60-trading-partners-14035724.html,2026-08-04 01:22:02
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Balochistan declares August 11 Independence Day, seeks global recognition","Balochistan declares August 11 Independence Day, seeks global recognition","Balochistan declares August 11 Independence Day, seeks global recognition Baloch leader Mir Yar Baloch appealed to people across the region and the diaspora to hoist the flag of what the group calls an independent Balochistan at homes, markets, educational institutions and other public places Baloch leader Mir Yar Baloch appealed to people across the region and the diaspora to hoist the flag of what the group calls an independent Balochistan at homes, markets, educational institutions and other public places Republic of Balochistan on Monday called for global celebrations on August 11, declaring the date as Balochistan's Independence Day and renewing its appeal for international recognition of what it describes as a sovereign Baloch state. The announcement comes weeks after the group proclaimed Balochistan's independence from Pakistan, intensifying its campaign for global support. In a statement, the Republic of Balochistan urged Baloch communities around the world to commemorate August 11 with demonstrations of national unity and solidarity. Baloch leader Mir Yar Baloch appealed to people across the region and the diaspora to hoist the flag of what the group calls an independent Balochistan at homes, markets, educational institutions and other public places. According to the statement, the observance is intended to preserve the historical significance of the date and pass it on to future generations. The group said August 11 would be observed every year as the national day of an independent Balochistan. The statement claimed that the partition of the Indian subcontinent triggered decades of conflict, political instability, terrorism and economic crises across South Asia. It further alleged that Pakistan's policies had fuelled instability both within the region an","Tue, 4 Aug 2026 00:43:20 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/balochistan-declares-august-11-independence-day-seeks-global-recognition-14035714.html,2026-08-04 01:22:02
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Pentagon strikes deals to boost Patriot, THAAD production amid strain on stocks due to Ukraine, Iran wars","Pentagon strikes deals to boost Patriot, THAAD production amid strain on stocks due to Ukraine, Iran wars","Pentagon strikes deals to boost Patriot, THAAD production amid strain on stocks due to Ukraine, Iran wars The Pentagon has struck agreements with Lockheed Martin and Northrop Grumman to ramp up production of Patriot and THAAD missile defence systems as conflicts in Ukraine and Iran put pressure on US weapons stockpiles. The Pentagon has struck agreements with Lockheed Martin and Northrop Grumman to ramp up production of Patriot and THAAD missile defence systems as conflicts in Ukraine and Iran put pressure on US weapons stockpiles. The US Department of Defense has signed framework agreements with Lockheed Martin and Northrop Grumman to significantly increase production of components used in the Patriot PAC-3 and Terminal High Altitude Area Defense (THAAD) missile interceptor systems. The move comes as conflicts in Ukraine and the Middle East have placed growing pressure on US weapons stocks, prompting the Pentagon to push defence contractors to accelerate production and replenish critical missile inventories. According to the Pentagon, the new framework deal is expected to enable a threefold increase in Patriot production and a fourfold rise in THAAD system output. The agreement will also establish a second supplier for PAC-3 solid rocket motors and increase production of ignition safety devices, key components used in the missile defence systems. The Pentagon has been pressing defence companies to move faster on production agreements reached earlier this year as demand for advanced air-defence systems continues to rise. The latest agreement follows a major contract awarded by the US Army to Lockheed Martin last week. The contract, valued at up to $58.6 billion, covers the production of Patriot interceptor missiles. Patriot systems have become particularly important in Ukraine, where they ar","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 23:48:05 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pentagon-strikes-deals-to-boost-patriot-thaad-production-amid-strain-on-stocks-due-to-ukraine-iran-wars-14035717.html,2026-08-04 01:22:02
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,‘We want to take it into mining’: Jaishankar pitches deeper India-Uzbekistan ties,‘We want to take it into mining’: Jaishankar pitches deeper India-Uzbekistan ties,"‘We want to take it into mining’: Jaishankar pitches deeper India-Uzbekistan ties During talks with Uzbek Foreign Minister Bakhtiyor Saidov in New Delhi, Jaishankar also called for greater trade and stronger engagement across sectors. During talks with Uzbek Foreign Minister Bakhtiyor Saidov in New Delhi, Jaishankar also called for greater trade and stronger engagement across sectors. India and Uzbekistan are looking to expand their strategic and economic cooperation, with New Delhi placing a particular focus on mining and critical rare earth minerals during high-level talks in New Delhi. The two sides also discussed opportunities to boost trade and cooperation in sectors including digital technology, energy, healthcare, education and pharmaceuticals. The discussions took place during Uzbek Foreign Minister Bakhtiyor Saidov’s four-day official visit to India from August 2 to 5. Saidov held talks with External Affairs Minister S Jaishankar and also met President Droupadi Murmu at Rashtrapati Bhavan. Welcoming Saidov, Jaishankar described Uzbekistan as an important part of India’s extended neighbourhood and highlighted the steady expansion of bilateral ties. He noted that India and Uzbekistan have maintained regular political and institutional exchanges, alongside longstanding movement of people, ideas and goods between the two countries. Jaishankar said bilateral cooperation has expanded across several sectors, including construction, digital technology, energy, healthcare and education. He added that India now wants to take the relationship further by exploring greater cooperation in mining. Trade between India and Uzbekistan currently stands at around $1 billion, with Jaishankar saying there is significan","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 23:24:58 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/we-want-to-take-it-into-mining-jaishankar-pitches-deeper-india-uzbekistan-ties-14035716.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'Unbelievably duplicitous': Trump accuses Iran of lying about US talks,'Unbelievably duplicitous': Trump accuses Iran of lying about US talks,"'Unbelievably duplicitous': Trump accuses Iran of lying about US talks The US president on Monday claimed Tehran privately sought negotiations despite publicly denying direct talks, while Iran maintained discussions are limited to Oman over the Strait of Hormuz The US president on Monday claimed Tehran privately sought negotiations despite publicly denying direct talks, while Iran maintained discussions are limited to Oman over the Strait of Hormuz US President Donald Trump on Monday accused Iran's leadership of acting in bad faith after Tehran denied that direct negotiations with Washington were underway, insisting discussions were limited to Oman over the Strait of Hormuz. In a post on his Truth Social platform, Trump claimed Iranian officials had privately sought talks with the US while publicly denying that any negotiations were taking place. ""Iranian Leadership is unbelievably duplicitous! They ask for a meeting, some would say 'beg,' talks begin, with more scheduled in the immediate future, and they say, openly and proudly, that they’re not having any discussions, that nothing is being talked about, and they’re only dealing with 'Oman,'"" Trump wrote. The US president also rejected Iran's assertion that it controls navigation through the Strait of Hormuz, arguing that the strategic waterway remains under US military control. ""They then go on to give their usual blather in saying, the Strait of Hormuz will be operated powerfully by them, when it is already completely controlled by the United States Navy and our 'Blockade' or, as some say, 'The United States Wall of Steel!'"" he said. Trump further warned that no shipments would reach Iran unless an agreement was reached. ""Nothing gets through to Iran, unless we want it to, and no","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 23:03:07 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/unbelievably-duplicitous-trump-accuses-iran-of-lying-about-us-talks-14035708.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Bangladesh urges India to deny Sheikh Hasina permission to hold presser as envoy Trivedi meets PM adviser,Bangladesh urges India to deny Sheikh Hasina permission to hold presser as envoy Trivedi meets PM adviser,"Bangladesh urges India to deny Sheikh Hasina permission to hold presser as envoy Trivedi meets PM adviser The issue was raised during a meeting between Indian High Commissioner Dinesh Trivedi and Bangladesh’s Foreign Affairs Adviser Humaiun Kobir in Dhaka. The issue was raised during a meeting between Indian High Commissioner Dinesh Trivedi and Bangladesh’s Foreign Affairs Adviser Humaiun Kobir in Dhaka. Bangladesh has urged India to prevent deposed prime minister Sheikh Hasina from making political statements from Indian soil, emphasising that any such activity could undermine the recent positive momentum in ties between the two countries. The issue was raised during a meeting between Bangladesh’s Foreign Affairs Adviser Humaiun Kobir and newly appointed Indian High Commissioner to Dhaka Dinesh Trivedi at the Prime Minister’s Office in Tejgaon on Monday. According to Bangladesh’s Foreign Ministry, the two sides discussed reports circulating on social media thatHasina could make a public address on August 5, the anniversary of her ouster from power in 2024. Kobir told Trivedi that Bangladesh expected India to ensure that Hasina, or anyone associated with banned organisations, does not use Indian territory to make political statements or undertake activities that could contribute to instability in Bangladesh. The adviser warned that allowing such activities could have an adverse impact on bilateral relations. Bangladesh also called for India to prevent individuals linked to Hasina’s disbanded Awami League and its affiliated organisations from using its territory for political activities directed at Bangladesh. The Indian High Commissioner assured Kobir that the concerns would be given due consideration and that necessary inquiri","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 22:31:53 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/bangladesh-asks-india-to-stop-sheikh-hasina-from-making-political-statements-as-envoy-trivedi-meets-pm-adviser-14035711.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Ceuta returns to calm but grapples with border surge aftermath,Ceuta returns to calm but grapples with border surge aftermath,"Ceuta returns to calm but grapples with border surge aftermath Most of the 50,000-60,000 migrants who crossed into Spain's North African enclave have returned to Morocco, but the deadly border breach has reignited Europe's immigration debate Most of the 50,000-60,000 migrants who crossed into Spain's North African enclave have returned to Morocco, but the deadly border breach has reignited Europe's immigration debate The Spanish enclave of Ceuta began returning to normal over the weekend after witnessing one of the largest migrant crossings in recent years, although residents remained shaken by the events that unfolded earlier in the week. Most of the estimated 50,000 to 60,000 migrants who entered the North African enclave by swimming, climbing or forcing their way across the border had returned to Morocco by Sunday. However, some migrants remained in the hills surrounding the city, while Spanish authorities continued searching coastal waters for bodies near the beach where the mass crossing took place. Businesses reopened across Ceuta, and residents and tourists gradually returned to restaurants, cafés and public spaces as daily life resumed in the city of about 84,000 people. Spanish authorities said at least 72 migrants were killed during the incident. Some drowned while attempting to reach the enclave, while others died in a stampede near a breakwater barrier used during the crossing. Moroccan authorities separately reported 11 deaths on their side of the border, although it was not immediately clear whether those fatalities were included in Spain's figures. Morocco blamed misinformation and human trafficking networks for the mass crossing and said an investigation into the incident was underway. Despite the","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 21:28:15 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/ceuta-returns-to-calm-but-grapples-with-border-surge-aftermath-14035700.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Iran rejects Trump's claim of US talks, says negotiations only with Oman on Hormuz","Iran rejects Trump's claim of US talks, says negotiations only with Oman on Hormuz","Iran rejects Trump's claim of US talks, says negotiations only with Oman on Hormuz The denial came after Trump said on Sunday that negotiations with Iran would resume on Monday as part of efforts to end the war, adding that he had postponed new military strikes to give diplomacy another chance The denial came after Trump said on Sunday that negotiations with Iran would resume on Monday as part of efforts to end the war, adding that he had postponed new military strikes to give diplomacy another chance Iran on Monday dismissed US President Donald Trump's claim that fresh negotiations between Washington and Tehran were set to begin, insisting there were currently no direct talks with the US despite the conflict entering its sixth month. The denial came after Trump said on Sunday that negotiations with Iran would resume on Monday as part of efforts to end the war, adding that he had postponed new military strikes to give diplomacy another chance. The US and Iran have remained at war since February 28, when Washington and Israel launched surprise attacks on Iranian targets. While intermittent diplomatic efforts have occasionally eased tensions, fighting has continued. Last week, Trump warned that the US could strike Iran ""very hard"", reportedly weighing attacks on the country's energy infrastructure. However, he later softened his stance, saying on Saturday that the ""perimeters"" of a possible agreement had already been established. Iran's Foreign Ministry rejected suggestions that direct negotiations with Washington were underway. ""We are not currently negotiating with the United States. Our negotiations are with Oman to secure passage through the Strait of Hormuz,""CNNquoted Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmaeil Baqaei as saying during a weekly press briefing. Baqaei desc","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 20:03:46 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/iran-rejects-trumps-claim-of-us-talks-says-negotiations-only-with-oman-on-hormuz-14035685.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Baloch group claims 97 Pakistani troops killed in July in operations across Balochistan,Baloch group claims 97 Pakistani troops killed in July in operations across Balochistan,"Baloch group claims 97 Pakistani troops killed in July in operations across Balochistan The Balochistan Liberation Front (BLF) has claimed that 97 Pakistani military personnel were killed and 15 others seriously injured in 48 operations across Balochistan in July The Balochistan Liberation Front (BLF) has claimed that 97 Pakistani military personnel were killed and 15 others seriously injured in 48 operations across Balochistan in July The Balochistan Liberation Front (BLF) has claimed that it killed 97 Pakistani military personnel and seriously wounded 15 others during 48 operations carried out across Balochistan in July, according toANIreport citing The Balochistan Post (TBP). The armed group also claimed responsibility for killing five people it described as informants and agents during the month. In its monthly operational report, BLF spokesperson Major Gwahram Baloch said the group carried out attacks targeting Pakistani military and state forces, military and economic infrastructure, communication systems and surveillance networks. The alleged operations were conducted in 23 locations, including Mastung, Khuzdar, Basima, Mand, Tump, Kahan, Chagai, Kharan, Kalat, Quetta, Zamuran, Surab, Mashkel, Buleda, Karachi, Pasni, Rakhni, Naal, Khudan, Balnegwar and Mashkay. The BLF said its operations were aimed at weakening what it termed Pakistan's ""colonial structure"" in Balochistan, preventing the exploitation of the region's resources and curbing the state's military presence. According to the BLF, the most significant operation took place on July 22, when its fighters attacked a Pakistani military convoy at Macro Stop in the Khadkocha area of Mastung. Major Gwahram Baloch claimed that 17 military personnel were killed and 10 others in","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 18:20:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/baloch-group-claims-97-pakistani-troops-killed-in-july-in-operations-across-balochistan-14035686.html,2026-08-04 01:22:03
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Wildfires sweeping France, Spain have cost Europe $3.6 billion this year, says report","Wildfires sweeping France, Spain have cost Europe $3.6 billion this year, says report","Wildfires sweeping France, Spain have cost Europe $3.6 billion this year, says report Wildfires and drought have scorched nearly 500,000 hectares across Europe, disrupting industries, agriculture, transport and energy supplies, while experts warn the true economic toll will be far higher Wildfires and drought have scorched nearly 500,000 hectares across Europe, disrupting industries, agriculture, transport and energy supplies, while experts warn the true economic toll will be far higher Wildfires and heatwaves across France, Spain and several other European countries have already caused economic losses exceeding €3 billion ($3.51 billion) this year, underscoring the growing financial impact of climate change-fuelled disasters. According toFinancial Timesestimates, nearly 500,000 hectares of land have been destroyed during the first two months of this year's wildfire season. The restoration cost for the five worst-affected Eurozone countries — France, Spain, Portugal, Greece and Romania — has reached around €3.1 billion, already surpassing the European Commission's estimated annual average loss of €2.5 billion ($2.9 billion) for the entire European Union. The estimates are based on the European Commission's methodology and similar calculations used by the French government. They measure the cost of restoring burned land to its previous condition, including reforestation and long-term maintenance, with costs varying depending on forest type, land cover and the age of vegetation. However, experts warn that the actual economic damage will be significantly higher. According toFT, insurance claims for damaged homes and businesses are still being processed, while farmers continue to assess crop losses. The tourism industry, which is at its busiest during summer, has","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 17:58:14 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/wildfires-sweeping-france-spain-have-cost-europe-3-6-billion-this-year-says-report-14035666.html,2026-08-04 01:22:04
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,How an Iran-linked network is turning teenagers into hitmen across Europe,How an Iran-linked network is turning teenagers into hitmen across Europe,"How an Iran-linked network is turning teenagers into hitmen across Europe Europe's Foxtrot Network is accused of recruiting teenagers through social media to carry out murders and attacks, with investigators alleging links to Iran. From encrypted instructions to cross-border assassination plots, police believe the network has been linked to around 35 murders since 2022 Europe's Foxtrot Network is accused of recruiting teenagers through social media to carry out murders and attacks, with investigators alleging links to Iran. From encrypted instructions to cross-border assassination plots, police believe the network has been linked to around 35 murders since 2022 For years, organised crime groups across Europe have relied on experienced criminals to carry out contract killings and violent attacks. Investigators now say that model has changed dramatically. Instead of recruiting seasoned gang members, criminal organisations are increasingly turning to teenagers, identifying vulnerable young people through social media, promising them large sums of money and directing them to carry out murders using encrypted messaging applications, said aBBCreport published on Saturday. At the centre of one of Europe's most extensive investigations is Sweden's Foxtrot Network, an organised crime syndicate that police believe has transformed murder-for-hire into what Europol describes as ""violence-as-a-service"" (VaaS). Intelligence gathered by police in several countries suggests that after its leader, Rawa Majid, relocated to Tehran, elements of the network were allegedly used to target individuals regarded by Iran as enemies, including dissidents, journalists and Israeli-linked interests. Iran has categorically denied any involvement with the gang. The Foxtrot Network first gained notoriety in Sweden through its involvement in gang violence and drug traffickin","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 17:54:24 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/how-iran-linked-foxtrot-network-is-turning-teenagers-into-hitmen-europe-14035633.html,2026-08-04 01:22:04
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Pakistan deploys Chinese SH-15 artillery guns near India border: Report,Pakistan deploys Chinese SH-15 artillery guns near India border: Report,"Pakistan deploys Chinese SH-15 artillery guns near India border: Report Pakistan has deployed a large number of Chinese-made SH-15 wheeled self-propelled artillery guns along sectors facing India, significantly strengthening its long-range firepower near the border Pakistan has deployed a large number of Chinese-made SH-15 wheeled self-propelled artillery guns along sectors facing India, significantly strengthening its long-range firepower near the border Pakistan has deployed a large number of Chinese-made SH-15 wheeled self-propelled artillery guns along sectors facing India, significantly strengthening its long-range firepower near the border, according to aTimes of Indiareport. The SH-15, known as the PCL-181 in China, is a 155mm, 52-calibre artillery system manufactured by Chinese defence company Norinco. Pakistan ordered 236 of these guns in 2019, with deliveries beginning in 2022 and a second batch arriving in 2023. According to the report, Islamabad has also secured a Transfer of Technology (ToT) agreement to manufacture the gun locally at the Heavy Industries Taxila facility. According to the report, most of the SH-15 guns have been deployed with Pakistani formations facing India, particularly in Gujranwala, Sialkot and Jalalpur Jattan, located behind the strategically important Shakargarh bulge. The region lies south of Jammu and north of Amritsar, with Pathankot to its east. The report also claims that these artillery systems were deployed during Operation Sindoor. In northern Punjab and Pakistan-occupied Kashmir, the SH-15 has become the primary self-propelled artillery platform, while desert sectors opposite Rajasthan and Gujarat continue to rely mainly on US-origin M-109 tracked self-propelled guns, which are considered mo","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 17:49:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pakistan-deploys-chinese-sh-15-artillery-guns-near-india-border-report-14035680.html,2026-08-04 01:22:04
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Russia hits Zaporizhzhia with 8 glide bombs in 90 minutes — Ukraine lacks effective counter,Russia hits Zaporizhzhia with 8 glide bombs in 90 minutes — Ukraine lacks effective counter,"Russia hits Zaporizhzhia with 8 glide bombs in 90 minutes — Ukraine lacks effective counter The strikes damaged 22 apartment buildings across four districts, as Russia and Ukraine continued to exchange drone and missile attacks overnight. The strikes damaged 22 apartment buildings across four districts, as Russia and Ukraine continued to exchange drone and missile attacks overnight. Russian glide bombs struck residential areas of the southern Ukrainian city of Zaporizhzhia in a concentrated attack Sunday evening, killing one person and injuring 31 others, according to news agencyAPciting regional governor Ivan Fedorov. Eight bombs were dropped within about 90 minutes, damaging 22 apartment buildings across four districts, Fedorov said. The victims included a 71-year-old woman who was killed and a 16-year-old girl among those wounded. Russia has increasingly relied on glide bombs against Ukrainian cities since launching its full-scale invasion more than four years ago. The weapons are Soviet-era bombs modified with guidance systems, allowing them to strike targets from a distance. Some of the bombs can carry up to 3,000 kilograms (6,600 pounds) of explosives, producing massive craters and extensive destruction. Ukraine has struggled to develop an effective defence against the weapons. The latest attack adds to the growing toll on civilian areas as Russia continues to use glide bombs, drones and ballistic missiles against Ukrainian targets. Alongside the glide-bomb attacks, Russian ballistic missiles remain a major threat to Ukrainian cities. President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has again urged Western allies to supply additional U.S.-made Patriot air defence systems. Zelenskyy said Patriot interceptors are among the f","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 18:01:37 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/russia-hits-zaporizhzhia-with-8-glide-bombs-in-90-minutes-ukraine-lacks-effective-counter-taking-14035675.html,2026-08-04 01:22:04
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US military crowdsourcing new ‘creative’ ideas to pressure Iran as Trump extends campaign,US military crowdsourcing new ‘creative’ ideas to pressure Iran as Trump extends campaign,"US military crowdsourcing new ‘creative’ ideas to pressure Iran as Trump extends campaign The move reflects growing concern within Washington that months of military strikes have failed to achieve their core objectives, with Iran refusing to back down over its nuclear programme or its position on the strategically vital Strait of Hormuz The move reflects growing concern within Washington that months of military strikes have failed to achieve their core objectives, with Iran refusing to back down over its nuclear programme or its position on the strategically vital Strait of Hormuz As US President Donald Trump weighs his next move in the ongoing conflict with Iran, the US military has turned to an unusual approach — asking its own analysts for fresh ideas on how to increase pressure on Tehran. The move reflects growing concern within Washington that months of military strikes have failed to achieve their core objectives, with Iran refusing to back down over its nuclear programme orits position on the strategically vital Strait of Hormuz. Even as Trump has repeatedlythreatened tougher military action, his administration has continued to explore diplomatic options amid regional efforts to prevent a wider war. According to aCNNreport, a senior officer in the intelligence branch of US Central Command (Centcom) recently sent an email to a broad group of military analysts seeking ""creative and unconventional"" ways to punish Iran. The email, sent by a senior Centcom officer, urged analysts to come up with new approaches that could pressure Iran beyond the military strategies already under consideration. According to people familiar with the mail, the crowdsourcing exercise was unusual within the US military and reflected an acknowledgement that the current strategy requires reassessment. Responding to questions abou","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 15:47:41 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-military-crowdsourcing-new-creative-ideas-to-pressure-iran-as-trump-extends-campaign-14035639.html,2026-08-04 01:22:05
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,What is a visa bond and will it affect Indians as Trump administration hikes fee for 50 countries?,What is a visa bond and will it affect Indians as Trump administration hikes fee for 50 countries?,"What is a visa bond and will it affect Indians as Trump administration hikes fee for 50 countries? Amid the Trump administration's crackdown on immigration, a new worry has emerged about whether Indians will now have to deposit up to $20,000 to get a US tourist visa. But should the measure worry Indian travellers? Amid the Trump administration's crackdown on immigration, a new worry has emerged about whether Indians will now have to deposit up to $20,000 to get a US tourist visa. But should the measure worry Indian travellers? Amid the Trump administration's crackdown on immigration, a new worry has emerged about whether Indians will now have to deposit up to $20,000 to get a US tourist visa. The root of the confusion was a statement by the US State Department, which announced that it would make its visa bond programme permanent from August 3, 2026. The measure entails that certain applicants for B-1 (business) and B-2 (tourist) visas may be asked to deposit a refundable bond of up to $20,000 before the visa to the United States is issued. The programme in question now applies to 30 nations, mostly from Africa and Asia. So here's what the concept of a visa bond is, and should Indians be worried? It is pertinent to note that a visa bond is like a refundable security deposit. A consular officer can ask an applicant from the listed country to deposit $10,000, $15,000 or up to $20,000 before receiving a B-1/B-2 visa. It is pertinent to note that this money is not a visa fee and does not guarantee visa approval. If the visa is refused, the bond is returned in its entirety. On the other hand, if the visa is granted and a traveller leaves the United States within the authorised period, the bond is refunded after compliance. However, if the visa holder overstays their visit or violates any con","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 14:49:49 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/what-is-a-visa-bond-and-will-it-affect-indians-as-trump-administration-hikes-fee-for-50-countries-14035590.html,2026-08-04 01:22:06
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Has Pakistan blocked Al Jazeera over its PoK protest coverage?,Has Pakistan blocked Al Jazeera over its PoK protest coverage?,"Has Pakistan blocked Al Jazeera over its PoK protest coverage? Pakistan is facing allegations of restricting access to Al Jazeera after the broadcaster extensively reported on protests, election-related unrest and a security crackdown in Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir (PoK). While no official ban has been announced, activists and opposition leaders linked the access issue to its reporting Pakistan is facing allegations of restricting access to Al Jazeera after the broadcaster extensively reported on protests, election-related unrest and a security crackdown in Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir (PoK). While no official ban has been announced, activists and opposition leaders linked the access issue to its reporting Questions are mounting over media freedom in Pakistan after claims that international broadcasterAl Jazeerabecame inaccessible to users across the country following its extensive reporting on the ongoing protests and election-related violence in Pakistan-Occupied Kashmir (PoK). Although Islamabad has not officially acknowledged blocking the Qatari news network, activists, opposition leaders and social media users have alleged that access toAl JazeeraEnglish was restricted soon after the broadcaster published a series of ground reports from Muzaffarabad highlighting the security crackdown on civilians. The restrictions have escalated concerns over press freedom at a time when Pakistan is facing growing criticism over its handling of unrest in PoK. Reports have also suggested that access to several local and regional digital news platforms has been curtailed alongside international broadcasters, though Pakistani authorities have not publicly confirmed any such action. The controversy comes amid weeks of protests, communication blackouts and clashes between demonstrators and security forces in the region. Reports of restricted access first surfaced on social me","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 14:26:15 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/has-pakistan-blocked-al-jazeera-over-pok-protests-everything-there-is-to-know-14035620.html,2026-08-04 01:22:06
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Vishwesh Negi appointed India's next ambassador to Iran amid heightened West Asia tensions,Vishwesh Negi appointed India's next ambassador to Iran amid heightened West Asia tensions,"Vishwesh Negi appointed India's next ambassador to Iran amid heightened West Asia tensions Negi, a 2002-batch officer of the Indian Foreign Service (IFS), is currently serving as a Joint Secretary within the Ministry of External Affairs in New Delhi Negi, a 2002-batch officer of the Indian Foreign Service (IFS), is currently serving as a Joint Secretary within the Ministry of External Affairs in New Delhi India has appointed senior diplomat Vishwesh Negi as its next Ambassador to the Islamic Republic of Iran, the Ministry of External Affairs (MEA) announced on Monday. Negi, a 2002-batch Indian Foreign Service (IFS) officer, is currently serving as a Joint Secretary in the MEA and is expected to take up his new assignment in Tehran shortly. His appointment comes at a crucial time as tensions remain high in West Asia following recent military developments and renewed diplomatic efforts involving the United States and Iran. India has been closely monitoring the situation, particularly due to concerns over the safety of commercial shipping and Indian seafarers in the strategically important Strait of Hormuz. The MEA said Negi will soon assume charge in Tehran, where he will oversee India's diplomatic engagement with Iran at a time when regional security remains fragile. India and Iran share longstanding ties in areas such as energy, trade, connectivity and regional cooperation. The appointment also comes as New Delhi continues to stress the need for dialogue and diplomacy to prevent further escalation in the region. The security of sea lanes in the Gulf remains a major concern for India, given its dependence on energy imports and maritime trade passing through the Strait of Hormuz. The announcement follows External Affairs M","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:37:41 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/india/vishwesh-negi-appointed-indias-next-ambassador-to-iran-amid-heightened-west-asia-tensions-14035615.html,2026-08-04 01:22:06
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Myanmar's Aung San Suu Kyi meets Red Cross official in rare appearance since 2021 coup,Myanmar's Aung San Suu Kyi meets Red Cross official in rare appearance since 2021 coup,"Myanmar's Aung San Suu Kyi meets Red Cross official in rare appearance since 2021 coup Myanmar's government on Monday announced that detained former leader Aung San Suu Kyi had met a representative of the Red Cross, marking one of her rare interactions with the outside world since the military seized power in 2021 Myanmar's government on Monday announced that detained former leader Aung San Suu Kyi had met a representative of the Red Cross, marking one of her rare interactions with the outside world since the military seized power in 2021 Myanmar's military-backed government on Monday announced that detained former leader Aung San Suu Kyi had met a representative of the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC), marking one of her rare interactions with the outside world since the military seized power in 2021. The announcement was accompanied by photographs showing the Nobel Peace Prize laureate shaking hands with Arnaud de Baecque, the ICRC's representative in Myanmar, during a meeting in the capital. The government did not reveal where the meeting took place or what was discussed. The images offered one of the few public glimpses of Suu Kyi in recent years, as she has remained largely out of public view since being detained following the military coup that overthrew her elected government. Among the photographs released by the Information Bureau, and reported byAssociated Press, were images of Suu Kyi cutting a pink birthday cake carrying the message, ""Happy Birthday Aunty Suu, 19.6.2026."" The pictures appeared to have been taken during celebrations marking her 81st birthday in June. In the images, Suu Kyi appeared relaxed and smiling, with no visible signs of poor health. However, her medical condition could not be independently verified from the photographs. The ICRC's Myanm","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:04:01 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/myanmars-aung-san-suu-kyi-meets-red-cross-official-in-rare-appearance-since-2021-coup-14035591.html,2026-08-04 01:22:06
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Maduro sends message from prison;  welcomes ‘any path to dialogue’ in Venezuela,Maduro sends message from prison;  welcomes ‘any path to dialogue’ in Venezuela,"Maduro sends message from prison;  welcomes ‘any path to dialogue’ in Venezuela Venezuela’s imprisoned former President Nicolas Maduro on Saturday welcomed ""any path of dialogue"" as the Latin American nation's government prepares for a transition from an interim set-up. Venezuela’s imprisoned former President Nicolas Maduro on Saturday welcomed ""any path of dialogue"" as the Latin American nation's government prepares for a transition from an interim set-up. Venezuela’s imprisoned former President Nicolas Maduro on Saturday welcomed ""any path of dialogue"" as the Latin American nation's government prepares for a transition from an interim set-up. In the coming week, there will be a US-backed meeting between representatives of Venezuela's interim government and opposition leaders. The autocratic leader and his wife, Cilia Flores, were captured during a military raid on January 3. The couple are being held in a New York detention centre awaiting trial on charges related to drug and firearms trafficking. It is pertinent to note that both of them have pleaded not guilty. “We welcome any path to dialogue that helps consolidate peace, coexistence, and reconciliation among Venezuelans,” read a message posted on the official Telegram account of the deposed president. “To speak of a national renaissance as a common goal is to speak of mutual respect for diversity, of inclusion for everyone,” he added. It was not immediately clear exactly how Maduro posted his message from his jail cell. However, it is known that the former strongman leader is in regular contact with his lawyers as he awaits trial in US federal court. His son, Nicolas Maduro Guerra, who is a lawmaker, reposted the former president’s message on X, formerly known as Twitter. Shortly after","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:36:21 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/maduro-sends-message-from-prison-welcomes-any-path-to-dialogue-in-venezuela-14035548.html,2026-08-04 01:22:07
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Pakistan suicide bombing: Video captures exact moment blast rips through peace rally that killed 14,Pakistan suicide bombing: Video captures exact moment blast rips through peace rally that killed 14,"Pakistan suicide bombing: Video captures exact moment blast rips through peace rally that killed 14 The attacker detonated an explosive vest outside a police station after being stopped by security personnel, killing civilians and police officers gathered near an anti-militancy peace rally in Pakistan's Swat Valley The attacker detonated an explosive vest outside a police station after being stopped by security personnel, killing civilians and police officers gathered near an anti-militancy peace rally in Pakistan's Swat Valley At least 14 people were killed and more than two dozen injured after a suicide bomber detonated an explosive vest near an anti-militancy peace rally in Pakistan's northwestern Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province on Sunday. Theattacktook place in Kabal, a town in the Swat Valley, where hundreds of residents had gathered to demand stronger action against the resurgence of militant violence in the region. A video circulating on social media captured the moment the explosion ripped through the area, sending demonstrators fleeing as thick black smoke filled the sky. According to Swat District Police Officer Umar Khan, the suspected attacker attempted to enter the nearby Kabal Police Station compound through its main gate. Security personnel intercepted the suspect and began searching him before he detonated the explosive vest. ""The bomber blew himself up at the station gate after being stopped,"" Khan said. The explosion killed at least nine civilians participating in the rally and five police officers stationed at the security checkpoint. Emergency responders rushed dozens of injured people to nearby hospitals, where several remain in critical condition. Note: Firstpost could not independently verify the authenticity of the video. The demonstration, known locally as the Olasi","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:19:55 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pakistan-suicide-bombing-video-captures-exact-moment-blast-rips-through-peace-rally-that-killed-14-14035583.html,2026-08-04 01:22:07
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"PoK election second phase: PML-N bags 14 seats amid protests, military scrutiny and India's criticism","PoK election second phase: PML-N bags 14 seats amid protests, military scrutiny and India's criticism","PoK election second phase: PML-N bags 14 seats amid protests, military scrutiny and India's criticism The elections have been overshadowed by allegations of rigging, political unrest and violence, with India rejecting the exercise as an attempt to legitimise Pakistan's control over the region The elections have been overshadowed by allegations of rigging, political unrest and violence, with India rejecting the exercise as an attempt to legitimise Pakistan's control over the region Pakistan Muslim League-Nawaz (PML-N) emerged as the biggest winner in the second phase of the Pakistan-occupied Kashmir (PoK) Legislative Assembly elections, securing 14 of the 18 seats for which unofficial results were declared on Sunday. The Pakistan Peoples Party (PPP) won the remaining four seats, according to unofficial figures released by the Election Commission and reported by Pakistani newspaperDawn. Voting was scheduled in 20 constituencies, but polling in two seats — LA-27 Muzaffarabad-I (Kutla) and LA-28 Muzaffarabad-II (Lachhrat) — was postponed after landslides and bad weather blocked roads, preventing election officials and polling material from reaching the constituencies. The second phase follows thefirst round of polling held on July 27, while the final phase is scheduled for August 10. The elections have been overshadowed by allegations of rigging, political unrest and violence, withIndia rejecting the exerciseas an attempt to legitimise Pakistan's control over the region. The latest round of polling was particularly significant as all 12 reserved seats for refugees settled in mainland Pakistan went to the polls. These seats have become thefocus of widespread protests, with many local groups arguing that they distort political representation in the region. The Awami Act","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 12:14:52 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/pok-election-second-phase-pml-n-bags-14-seats-amid-protests-military-scrutiny-and-indias-criticism-14035579.html,2026-08-04 01:22:07
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Katz vs IDF: Israeli military rejects defense minister's announcement to replace West Bank chief,Katz vs IDF: Israeli military rejects defense minister's announcement to replace West Bank chief,"Katz vs IDF: Israeli military rejects defense minister's announcement to replace West Bank chief A rare public confrontation has erupted between Israel's political and military leadership after Defense Minister Israel Katz announced that he was replacing the head of the IDF' Central Command, only for the military to reject the move within hours A rare public confrontation has erupted between Israel's political and military leadership after Defense Minister Israel Katz announced that he was replacing the head of the IDF' Central Command, only for the military to reject the move within hours A rare public confrontation has erupted between Israel's political and military leadership after Defense Minister Israel Katz announced on live television that he was replacing the head of the Israel Defense Forces' Central Command, only for the military to reject the move within hours. Speaking toChannel 14on Sunday, Katz said Maj. Gen. Avi Bluth would be replaced by Maj. Gen. Dado Bar Kalifa, who currently heads the IDF Personnel Directorate. However, according to aTimes of Israelreport, the IDF has responded that Bluth would remain in his position, adding that no decision had been made by Chief of Staff Lt. Gen. Eyal Zamir to replace him. The dispute has exposed growing tensions within Israel's leadership and triggered sharp reactions from opposition leaders, while far-right politicians welcomed Katz's announcement. Katz accused Bluth of acting against government policy by seeking to renew an administrative restraining order against Tal Yinon Dardik, an Israeli settler suspected of involvement in a violent attack on Palestinians in the West Bank earlier this year. Dardik has denied participating in the alleged assault. After violating restrictions placed on him, he was detained and has reportedly been on a hunger strike for wee","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:12:25 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/katz-vs-idf-israeli-military-rejects-defense-ministers-announcement-to-replace-west-bank-chief-14035564.html,2026-08-04 01:22:10
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Trump administration ramps up immigration crackdown as ICE detentions reach July record,Trump administration ramps up immigration crackdown as ICE detentions reach July record,"Trump administration ramps up immigration crackdown as ICE detentions reach July record The US Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) recorded its highest monthly detention figures of Trump's second term in July, as the administration stepped up its campaign against illegal immigration The US Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) recorded its highest monthly detention figures of Trump's second term in July, as the administration stepped up its campaign against illegal immigration The US Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) recorded its highest monthly detention figures of President Donald Trump's second term in July, as the administration stepped up its campaign against illegal immigration across the country. According to internal Department of Homeland Security (DHS) data, obtained byCBS News, more than 46,000 people facing deportation were taken into ICE custody during July. The figure includes migrants accused of entering the United States illegally or overstaying their visas. The latest numbers indicate that immigration enforcement continued to intensify over the summer, with the White House pushing federal authorities to significantly increase arrests as part of Trump's pledge to carry out what he has described as the largest deportation operation in American history. The July total surpassed the roughly 43,000 detentions recorded in June, which had previously been the highest monthly figure of Trump's current term. The data also showed that ICE admitted an average of around 1,500 detainees each day during July, although some had initially been arrested by Customs and Border Protection officers. As of the weekend, about 68,000 people were being held in ICE detention facilities nationwide, approaching the record level of more than 70,000 detain","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 10:13:11 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-administration-ramps-up-immigration-crackdown-as-ice-detentions-reach-july-record-14035556.html,2026-08-04 01:22:10
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Suspect in Idaho shooting dies of self-inflicted gunshot wounds; probe underway,Suspect in Idaho shooting dies of self-inflicted gunshot wounds; probe underway,"Suspect in Idaho shooting dies of self-inflicted gunshot wounds; probe underway The man accused of opening fire at a fast-food restaurant in Idaho died of a self-inflicted gunshot wound. The authorities investigating the matter noted that quick action by an off-duty law enforcement officer and an armed bystander helped prevent even more bloodshed. The man accused of opening fire at a fast-food restaurant in Idaho died of a self-inflicted gunshot wound. The authorities investigating the matter noted that quick action by an off-duty law enforcement officer and an armed bystander helped prevent even more bloodshed. The man accused of opening fire at a fast-food restaurant in Idaho died of a self-inflicted gunshot wound. The authorities investigating the matter noted that quick action by an off-duty law enforcement officer and an armed bystander helped prevent even more bloodshed. Speaking on Sunday, Matthew Hicks, the Twin Falls police chief, said investigators are confident 24-year-old Chad Williams carried out Saturday’s attack alone. The devastating shooting incident left three people dead and seven others injured. Amid the investigation, officials maintained that two of the wounded have been treated and released from the hospital, three remain in stable condition, and two are still in critical condition. Hicks noted that an off-duty state trooper and an armed citizen, whose names have not been released, returned fire, causing the suspect to move away from the restaurant. “We believe their actions helped drive the suspect away from the scene, preventing further casualties,” the chief said. “We want to take a moment and commend the actions of the off-duty officer and the citizen, and their heroic action to stop this incident.” Detectives are continuing to investigate what led to the attack, with Hick adding that William","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 09:16:05 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/suspect-in-idaho-shooting-dies-of-self-inflicted-gunshot-wounds-probe-underway-14035526.html,2026-08-04 01:22:10
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Trump cancels strikes on Iran, says talks to resume: Decoding the big U-turn by the US president","Trump cancels strikes on Iran, says talks to resume: Decoding the big U-turn by the US president","Trump cancels strikes on Iran, says talks to resume: Decoding the big U-turn by the US president Within a span of two days, Donald Trump has gone from vowing to launch ‘very hard strikes’ against Iran to announcing that talks will resume on reaching a deal on the Strait of Hormuz. What led to this reversal? Was it pressure and frustration from the Gulf neighbours? Within a span of two days, Donald Trump has gone from vowing to launch ‘very hard strikes’ against Iran to announcing that talks will resume on reaching a deal on the Strait of Hormuz. What led to this reversal? Was it pressure and frustration from the Gulf neighbours? US President Donald Trump has made quite a few aggressive threats against Iran only to walk back on some of them. The latest instance came late last week. Last Friday (July 31), the American leader vowed to hit the Islamic Republic “very hard”. Later on Sunday (August 2), he announced that “we have just been asked by Iran, and other Middle Eastern Countries, to hold off any attack”, claiming that “the perimeters of a deal have been agreed to”. Iran’s state media vigorously denied that Tehran had made such a request at all. And while Iran rejected any claims of requesting Trump to hold off on strikes, Trump declared again that an agreement with Iran is imminent, with negotiations set to begin on Monday. If you are trying to catch up on the twists and turns in the ongoing US-Iran war, here’s a guide to what happened over the weekend and what led the US president to pause strikes on Iran. Last week, a fragile pause between the US and Iran ended afterTehran executed a surprise assault on American service membersstationed in Jordan, which prompted immediate US military retaliation. At a Camp David meeting, Trump expressed frustration over the stalled talks, accusing Iranian diplomats of being “very dishonourable” an","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 08:54:21 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/explainers/trump-iran-war-strikes-paused-talks-u-turn-gulf-nations-14035532.html,2026-08-04 01:22:11
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Bryan Kohberger’s former defence expert turns against him, backs death penalty","Bryan Kohberger’s former defence expert turns against him, backs death penalty","Bryan Kohberger’s former defence expert turns against him, backs death penalty A former digital forensics expert hired by Bryan Kohberger’s defence team has reversed his stance, saying new analysis of phone data convinced him the convicted Idaho murderer belongs on death row as Kohberger fights to withdraw his guilty plea A former digital forensics expert hired by Bryan Kohberger’s defence team has reversed his stance, saying new analysis of phone data convinced him the convicted Idaho murderer belongs on death row as Kohberger fights to withdraw his guilty plea A key digital forensics expert once hired to support Bryan Kohberger’s defence has made a dramatic reversal, saying he now believes the convicted Idaho murderer should face the death penalty. Sy Ray, a former Arizona homicide investigator and digital evidence specialist, made the comments days after Kohberger filed a handwritten petition seeking to withdraw his guilty plea in the killings of four University of Idaho students. Kohberger’s attempt to overturn the July 2025 plea deal could reopen the possibility of a death sentence, which was removed from consideration when he pleaded guilty. “Bring back the death penalty. I am all for this,” Ray said during a podcast interview with journalist Brian Entin. Ray said his changed opinion was based on a deeper review of Kohberger’s mobile phone records, which he claims contain more incriminating details than previously disclosed. According to Ray, advanced analysis of location data showed multiple “collisions” between Kohberger’s phone and the device belonging to victim Madison Mogen before the murders. Ray said he now believes Kohberger had been inside the victims’ home before the killings and that Mogen may have been the intended target. However, his current assessment contradicts his earli","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 08:35:51 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/bryan-kohbergers-former-defence-expert-turns-against-him-backs-death-penalty-14035533.html,2026-08-04 01:22:11
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,India map row: Glasgow eatery apologises after boxer Lovlina Borgohain flags missing Northeast,India map row: Glasgow eatery apologises after boxer Lovlina Borgohain flags missing Northeast,"India map row: Glasgow eatery apologises after boxer Lovlina Borgohain flags missing Northeast The controversy erupted after Olympic medallist Lovlina Borgohain noticed that a map of India printed on napkins and displayed outside the restaurant omitted the Northeast and inaccurately depicted Jammu and Kashmir during a celebratory dinner for the Commonwealth Games boxing contingent The controversy erupted after Olympic medallist Lovlina Borgohain noticed that a map of India printed on napkins and displayed outside the restaurant omitted the Northeast and inaccurately depicted Jammu and Kashmir during a celebratory dinner for the Commonwealth Games boxing contingent A celebratory dinner for India's record-breaking boxing contingent at the 2026 Commonwealth Games took an unexpected turn after a Glasgow-based Indian restaurant came under fire for displaying an inaccurate map of India in its logo. The restaurant, Mister Singh's India – The Home of Curry, later apologised and deactivated its official Instagram account after facing widespread criticism online. The incident occurred after India's boxing team gathered at the restaurant to celebrate its historic performance at the Glasgow Commonwealth Games, where the squad won 10 medals, including seven golds. Olympic and Commonwealth Games silver medallistLovlina Borgohain, who is from Assam, noticed that the restaurant's logo—printed on napkins and displayed outside the establishment—omitted India's Northeast. In a video recorded at the venue, Borgohain politely urged the management to correct the design. ""Please don't take this the wrong way. I felt a little hurt by this. In the map of India, our North East is missing... As someone from the North East, I can say that really hurts us."" The video quickly went viral on social media. Watch the video here: Boxing Federation of India President Ajay Singh also poin","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 07:58:47 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/india-map-row-glasgow-eatery-apologises-after-boxer-lovlina-borgohain-flags-missing-northeast-14035528.html,2026-08-04 01:22:11
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Is the UK running out of food? Farmers warn of an agricultural crisis as drought continues in parts of England,Is the UK running out of food? Farmers warn of an agricultural crisis as drought continues in parts of England,"Is the UK running out of food? Farmers warn of an agricultural crisis as drought continues in parts of England UK's National Farmers’ Union (NFU) warned that Britain could face shortages of some food amid a punishing drought in parts of England as the heatwave continues to batter the country. UK's National Farmers’ Union (NFU) warned that Britain could face shortages of some food amid a punishing drought in parts of England as the heatwave continues to batter the country. UK's National Farmers’ Union (NFU) warned that Britain could face shortages of some food amid a punishing drought in parts of England as the heatwave continues to batter the country. Amid the warnings, the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA) issued heat health alerts for eight regions of England, with temperatures set to rise above 30C in parts of the country. The British body maintained that the rising temperatures may increase health risks, particularly for vulnerable groups. The latest warnings are coming at a time when farms across the country were affected by floods over the winter, making planting extremely difficult, and now in many areas they do not have access to water as irrigation is being restricted due to drought. It is pertinent to note that the country is facing itsthird drought in the past five years, with the farmers calling the situation a ""new normal"" for the British agricultural industry. According to The Guardian, wheat farmers say their crop is half its normal height, and England is seeing its earliest ever harvest as farmers battle to salvage what they can of their produce. It is pertinent to note that in some parts of the United Kingdom, it has not rained for over a month, while multiple record-breaking heatwaves have hit the country since early summer, drying out the soil. ","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 07:27:10 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/is-the-uk-running-out-of-food-farmers-warn-of-an-agricultural-crisis-as-drought-continues-in-parts-of-england-14035517.html,2026-08-04 01:22:11
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"US Iran War Live Updates: Iran says no talks with US underway, Hormuz status won't change until strikes end","US Iran War Live Updates: Iran says no talks with US underway, Hormuz status won't change until strikes end","US Iran War Live Updates: Iran says no talks with US underway, Hormuz status won't change until strikes end US Iran War Live Updates: US President Donald Trump said negotiations with Iran will begin on Monday after shelving planned military strikes, as both sides pursue talks on the Strait of Hormuz and Tehran's nuclear programme US Iran War Live Updates: US President Donald Trump said negotiations with Iran will begin on Monday after shelving planned military strikes, as both sides pursue talks on the Strait of Hormuz and Tehran's nuclear programme US Iran War Live Updates: US President Donald Trump said an agreement with Iran was close and announced that negotiations between the two countries would resume on Monday, signalling renewed diplomatic momentum. His remarks came as Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian called on Washington to “remain committed” to the memorandum of understanding signed in June, saying the interim agreement “will be the centre of gravity” of Tehran’s “foreign relations in the future”. Meanwhile, Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said negotiations between Tehran and Oman over the management of the Strait of Hormuz were in their “final stages”. As diplomatic efforts gathered pace, fighting continued in Gaza, where Israeli forces carried out fresh bombardments that killed at least 18 Palestinians on Sunday, while health authorities reported a record rise in killings across the Strip during July. Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) on Monday claimed it had shot down an MQ-9 Reaper drone flying over the Strait of Hormuz. According to the IRGC-affiliatedSepah News, the unmanned aircraft was brought down by an air defence system operating over the strategic waterway, though the report did not specify where the drone had originated. While the origin of the aircraft ","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:33:15 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-iran-war-live-updates-trump-iran-airstrikes-middle-east-conflict-live-liveblog-14035521.html,2026-08-04 01:22:12
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Sudan drone strike on Darfur court kills 35, rights group blames army","Sudan drone strike on Darfur court kills 35, rights group blames army","Sudan drone strike on Darfur court kills 35, rights group blames army A drone strike on a traditional court in Sudan's North Darfur killed at least 35 people, according to a rights group, as fighting between the army and the RSF continued A drone strike on a traditional court in Sudan's North Darfur killed at least 35 people, according to a rights group, as fighting between the army and the RSF continued At least 35 people were killed after a drone strike hit a traditional court in Garra al-Zawaya village in North Darfur on Sunday, according to a Sudanese rights group, which blamed the attack on the Sudanese army. The strike targeted a gathering where civilians had assembled to attend local hearings, as fighting between the army and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces (RSF) continued into its third year. The Emergency Lawyers group, which has documented abuses by both the Sudanese army and the RSF throughout the conflict, said the strike took place at around 1:00 pm local time (1100 GMT). According to the organisation, the court in Garra al-Zawaya was hearing local disputes when it was struck. A witness from the area toldAFP, on condition of anonymity for safety reasons, ""The court session was being held under a tree with the participation of local community leaders."" The witness said those killed included ""four tribal leaders and two RSF commanders"". The village is under the control of the Rapid Support Forces, which have been fighting the Sudanese army since April 2023. There was no immediate response from the army. The RSF's self-declared government condemned the attack as ""a horrific crime"", claiming 37 people were killed and 50 others were injured. Garra al-Zawaya is located near Kabkabiya in North Darf","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 06:56:40 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/sudan-drone-strike-on-darfur-court-kills-35-rights-group-blames-army-14035513.html,2026-08-04 01:22:12
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,US Senate unveils stopgap spending bill to avert government shutdown until December 11,US Senate unveils stopgap spending bill to avert government shutdown until December 11,"US Senate unveils stopgap spending bill to avert government shutdown until December 11 The bipartisan proposal would keep federal agencies running through December 11, pushing a potential funding crisis until after the elections while delaying a controversial grants rule and resolving key disputes between Congress and the White House The bipartisan proposal would keep federal agencies running through December 11, pushing a potential funding crisis until after the elections while delaying a controversial grants rule and resolving key disputes between Congress and the White House In a bid to prevent another disruptive federal government shutdown ahead of the upcoming elections, key US senators have introduced a bipartisan stopgap spending bill that would fund federal agencies through December 11. The continuing resolution (CR), unveiled after days of negotiations between congressional leaders and the White House, is expected to be voted on later this week before lawmakers leave Washington for the traditional August recess. If approved by both chambers of Congress and signed into law by the President, the measure would delay a potentially contentious funding battle until after the election season. Government funding disputes in Washington are typically resolved at the last minute, often bringing the country close to a shutdown. This time, however, lawmakers have acted nearly two months before the current fiscal year ends on September 30. The move reflects growing concern among both Democrats and Republicans over the political and economic fallout of another government closure. The United States has already witnessed two record-breaking shutdowns within the past 10 months. Senate Majority LeaderJohn Thunesaid preventing another shutdown before Congress leaves for its August recess is his top priority. While t","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 06:49:38 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/us-senate-unveils-stopgap-spending-bill-to-avert-government-shutdown-until-december-11-14035512.html,2026-08-04 01:22:13
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,'You'll find out': Trump threatens to 'punish' Canada over the wildfires fiasco,'You'll find out': Trump threatens to 'punish' Canada over the wildfires fiasco,"'You'll find out': Trump threatens to 'punish' Canada over the wildfires fiasco US President Donald Trump once again repeated his threat against Canada over the wildfires fiasco that impacted parts of the United States as well. US President Donald Trump once again repeated his threat against Canada over the wildfires fiasco that impacted parts of the United States as well. US President Donald Trump once again repeated his threat against Canada over the wildfires fiasco that impacted parts of the United States as well. The American president on Sunday said that he has decided to ""punish"" Canada for the wildfires, and the punishment will be revealed soon. The remarks from the American leader came after a journalist on boardAir Force Oneasked Trump what measures he will take against Canada over the wildfire issue and if he has planned any punishment. ""I have. You'll find out,"" Trump responded, repeating his assertion that ""Canada must pay"". It is pertinent to note that for the longest time, Trump has beenthreatening to impose new tariffs on Canadaafter hundreds of wildfires have left much of the northern US covered by a blanket of smoke. The threats came after US lawmakers complained about the wildfires. ""The United States is being unnecessarily invaded by filthy, polluted, and unhealthy air,"" Trump said last month, threatening to impose new levies over Canada's ""willful negligence"". At that time, Trump said in his post on TruthSocial that he would call Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney to demand an explanation over his country's ""willful negligence,"" accusing the country of ""not properly maintaining"" their forests and brush. Amid the constant threats from the US, Canada's International Trade Ministe","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 06:45:24 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/youll-find-out-trump-threatens-to-punish-canada-over-the-wildfires-fiasco-14035506.html,2026-08-04 01:22:13
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Globe at a glance: From US-Iran talks to India receiving the 2030 Commonwealth Games baton,Globe at a glance: From US-Iran talks to India receiving the 2030 Commonwealth Games baton,"Globe at a glance: From US-Iran talks to India receiving the 2030 Commonwealth Games baton US-Iran peace talks and Washington's bid to avert a shutdown to Spain's migrant tragedy, Canada's airline strike, Venezuela's political reset and India taking the Commonwealth Games baton for Ahmedabad 2030—here are the top global developments you need to know today US-Iran peace talks and Washington's bid to avert a shutdown to Spain's migrant tragedy, Canada's airline strike, Venezuela's political reset and India taking the Commonwealth Games baton for Ahmedabad 2030—here are the top global developments you need to know today Trump to begin Iran talks amid Gulf tensions:US President Donald Trump said negotiations with Iran will begin Monday after shelving planned military strikes. The talks aim to reopen the Strait of Hormuz and curb Tehran's nuclear ambitions, even as Israel remains on alert and Gulf nations warn against renewed regional escalation. US Senate moves to avert government shutdown:US senators have unveiled a stopgap spending bill to keep federal agencies funded through December 11, aiming to avoid a pre-election government shutdown. The measure buys Congress more time to resolve spending disputes while delaying a controversial federal grants rule. WestJet strike disrupts Canada holiday travel:A pay dispute has triggered a strike by WestJet flight attendants, forcing the cancellation of hundreds of flights and disrupting travel for around 250,000 passengers during Canada's long weekend. Cabin crew are demanding pay for all hours worked, including pre- and post-flight duties. Trump bets on tech to counter drone terror:The Trump administration is launching private-sector “incubators” to accelerate anti-drone technologies as unmanned aerial threats grow worldwide. Officials say the initiative will fast-track innovation to det","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 06:29:46 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/globe-at-a-glance-from-us-iran-talks-to-india-receiving-the-2030-commonwealth-games-baton-14035509.html,2026-08-04 01:22:13
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Turkey slams Netanyahu's 'expansionist approach'; condemns Israel's latest strikes on Gaza,Turkey slams Netanyahu's 'expansionist approach'; condemns Israel's latest strikes on Gaza,"Turkey slams Netanyahu's 'expansionist approach'; condemns Israel's latest strikes on Gaza Amid the growing tensions in West Asia, Turkey on Sunday slammed Israel's latest strikes on Gaza that killed 17 people in the coastal enclave. Amid the growing tensions in West Asia, Turkey on Sunday slammed Israel's latest strikes on Gaza that killed 17 people in the coastal enclave. Amid the growing tensions in West Asia, Turkey on Sunday slammed Israel's latest strikes on Gaza that killed 17 people in the coastal enclave. Ankara's condemnation was directed towards Israel's Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, as it insisted that the government of the Jewish nation has ""no intention of making peace with Palestinians"". ""Netanyahu's sole objective is to displacePalestinians from their homelandand prevent the establishment of peace and stability in the region,"" the Foreign Ministry said in a statement on its official website. It also maintained that the Israeli premier continues to take all steps that undermine the fragile regional balance and sabotage mediation efforts. ""It has become imperative for the international community to adopt a more determined, consistent and robust stance against Netanyahu's expansionist and militarist approach, which seeks to turn the entire Middle East into a zone of conflict,"" the statement said. Turkey went on to call on countries around the world to support peace and prosperity for the Palestinians and wider West Asia. The Turkish foreign ministry urged the international community to act responsibly and uphold international law and humanitarian values. It is pertinent to note that the remarks from the Turkish foreign ministry came just hours after theIsraeli strikes killed at least 17 ","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 05:52:53 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/turkey-slams-netanyahus-expansionist-approach-condemns-israels-latest-strikes-on-gaza-14035501.html,2026-08-04 01:22:14
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Japan, US intervene to prop up yen after 40-year lows; Trump says it's a 'signal of friendship'","Japan, US intervene to prop up yen after 40-year lows; Trump says it's a 'signal of friendship'","Japan, US intervene to prop up yen after 40-year lows; Trump says it's a 'signal of friendship' Japan and the United States have carried out a rare coordinated intervention to support the yen after it hit 40-year lows. President Donald Trump called the move a ""signal of friendship"", while Tokyo warned it was prepared to take further action to stabilise the currency Japan and the United States have carried out a rare coordinated intervention to support the yen after it hit 40-year lows. President Donald Trump called the move a ""signal of friendship"", while Tokyo warned it was prepared to take further action to stabilise the currency Japan on Monday confirmed it had carried out a rare coordinated intervention with the United States to support the battered yen, marking the first such joint action in nearly 15 years as the Japanese currency came under intense pressure after falling to its weakest level in about four decades. The confirmation came after US President Donald Trump publicly acknowledged Washington's role, describing the move as a ""signal of friendship"" towards one of America's closest allies. ""They have a weakening yen, and they wanted a little bit of help. And we're always there for Japan,"" Trump told reporters aboard Air Force One. ""It was a signal of friendship,"" he added, saying the intervention would also help the global economy. In a statement issued on Monday, Japan's Finance Ministry said it conducted coordinated yen-buying intervention with the US Treasury on Friday to counter what it called ""excessive volatility and disorderly movements"" in the Japanese currency. Finance Minister Satsuki Katayama said Tokyo remained in close contact with Washington and was prepared to act again if market conditions warranted. ""The Japanese Ministry of Finance remains attentive and in close communication with our counterparts at the US","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 05:32:57 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/business/japan-us-intervene-prop-up-yen-40-year-lows-trump-signal-of-friendship-14035502.html,2026-08-04 01:22:14
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,Trump says negotiations with Iran will begin on Monday after calling off massive attack,Trump says negotiations with Iran will begin on Monday after calling off massive attack,"Trump says negotiations with Iran will begin on Monday after calling off massive attack US President Donald Trump said that new negotiations with Iran will begin Monday after he decided to hold off on striking the Islamic republic to pursue a deal to end the war entering its sixth month. US President Donald Trump said that new negotiations with Iran will begin Monday after he decided to hold off on striking the Islamic republic to pursue a deal to end the war entering its sixth month. US President Donald Trump said that new negotiations with Iran will begin Monday after he decided to hold off on striking the Islamic republic to pursue a deal to end the war entering its sixth month. Trump told reporters on Sunday that the talks would cover the Strait of Hormuz –- a route for global energy supplies that has become a key sticking point in the conflict -- and ultimately, the denuclearization of Iran. For its part, Iran said it was nearing a deal with Oman over a new route through the strait, where a tanker reported an explosion nearby on Sunday, underscoring the continued volatility of the strategic waterway. Oil prices fell on Monday at the opening of Asian trade after Trump announced the new negotiations, with West Texas Intermediate dropping 4.7 percent to $80.72 per barrel. Washington and Tehran have been at war since February 28, when the US and Israel launched surprise strikes against Iran, though months of on-off diplomacy have led to periods of relative calm. Following a return to strikes last month, fears had mounted that the fighting could escalate once again. Trump had threatened to hit Iran ""very hard"" and was reportedly considering renewed attacks including against energy infrastructure, with US embassies across the region on alert. Trump pulled back","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 05:24:58 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-says-negotiations-with-iran-will-begin-on-monday-after-calling-off-massive-attack-14035500.html,2026-08-04 01:22:14
Firstpost,India (Center-Right),en,"Israel, CentCom caught off guard as Trump announced Iran strike cancellation on Truth Social: Report","Israel, CentCom caught off guard as Trump announced Iran strike cancellation on Truth Social: Report","Israel, CentCom caught off guard as Trump announced Iran strike cancellation on Truth Social: Report Israeli officials and the US military’s Central Command (CentCom) were reportedly not informed in advance about President Donald Trump’s decision to call off a planned major strike on Iran, with Israeli officials learning of the move through his Truth Social post, according to Israeli media. Israeli officials and the US military’s Central Command (CentCom) were reportedly not informed in advance about President Donald Trump’s decision to call off a planned major strike on Iran, with Israeli officials learning of the move through his Truth Social post, according to Israeli media. Israeli officials and the US military’s Central Command (CentCom) were reportedly not given advance notice of US President Donald Trump’s decision to call off a planned major strike on Iran, according to Israeli media. Israel’sChannel 12reported that Israeli officials first became aware of Trump’s decision through his post on Truth Social. CentCom was also reportedly not informed beforehand. The development came despite Trump saying in his post that Israel had joined the US in a commitment to pursue a possible agreement with Iran. Trump said on Saturday that he had called off the planned attack after Iran and other Middle Eastern countries asked Washington to hold off, claiming that the broad outlines of a deal had been agreed. He said the proposed arrangement would include the “Immediate, complete, and total OPENING OF THE HORMUZ STRAIT” and an end to Iran’s nuclear threat. Trump also claimed that Israel had agreed to the move and called for a rapid conclusion to the negotiations. Iranian officials have rejected Trump’s account, calling his claim that Tehran sought a halt to the planned attacks a “lie”. Iranian military sources said the country’s armed forces remained at the highest leve","Mon, 3 Aug 2026 00:45:06 +0530",https://www.firstpost.com/world/israel-centcom-caught-off-guard-as-trump-announced-iran-strike-cancellation-on-truth-social-report-14035498.html,2026-08-04 01:22:15
